SET NOCOUNT ON;
SET XACT_ABORT ON;
DECLARE @unresolved INT;
BEGIN TRANSACTION;

;WITH raw AS (
    SELECT * FROM (VALUES
        (N'FG-DSE-ORIENT', N'WF-ORIENT-01', N'DSE-ORIENT', N'This is the first of eight huddles, and the one with no AI activity in it. Today is about what the motion is and what it asks of you. Frontier Accelerator exists because we are asking customers to reshape how they work around AI, and that argument is stronger when we have done it ourselves first. That is what Customer Zero means here: we apply these tools to our own real work, and we are straight about where they help and where they do not.', N'Weekly and short, led by the manager with a facilitator. The cadence carries the motion, so if the slot has to move, move it rather than drop it.
Four principles hold every week: keep the weekly rhythm, learn from what colleagues have actually tried, ground every session in real work rather than hypothetical scenarios, and try something between huddles so there is an experience to bring back.
Every huddle from next week runs to the same shape, and the agenda and timings are set out on the huddle page: a short framing of why the topic matters, then sharing what you tried since last time, then hands-on work on a real scenario, then a close where you pick the one thing you will carry into the week.
Five roles make the motion work. The Leader connects it to strategy and clears barriers. The Manager owns the huddle and opens with the why. The Facilitator runs it and draws every voice in. The Team Member experiments in their own workflow and brings back wins and friction. The Coach reviews patterns across huddles and feeds insight back.
The journey ahead: this week you get grounded, weeks two to eight you build the habit on real work, week nine the team decides which habits stick and what comes next, and from week ten you choose your own topics.
You are here as a peer, not an expert. You do not need prior AI experience, and “I do not know” is a good answer.
Two things on feedback. The facilitator posts one question in the chat each week, asking how useful the huddle was on a scale of one to ten. Huddle transcripts are reviewed for themes to improve the sessions, so keep confidential detail out of the room, whether that is customer, partner, enrollment or commercial, and put product problems into the tool''s own feedback control rather than here.', N'Which part of your week is assembling information rather than deciding anything?
Where do you already use AI, and where have you deliberately avoided it?
What would you need to see before you trusted an AI-drafted output in front of a customer?
What would make thirty minutes a week worth protecting for this?', N'Before the session: complete the baseline survey if it has been sent to you, confirm the huddle series is on your calendar, confirm you can open at least one of the tools named for your path, since the commitment at the end of this session runs in one of them, and come ready to describe the work you repeat most weeks. If your access is blocked, come anyway and say so, because that is a different problem from not being interested. No prior AI experience is needed for this session.', N'Which work do you repeat every week without ever thinking of it as a workflow?
Which of those steps is assembling information rather than making a decision?
Where does your judgment have to stay human no matter how good the output gets?
What would have to happen in these thirty minutes for you to want the next one?', N'Listen for unvoiced skepticism, for access blockers stated as disinterest, and for the assumption that this is a tool rollout. Listen for people describing only their exceptional work rather than the work they repeat, because the recurring and unglamorous tasks are the ones worth mapping. Listen for anyone who thinks they need to be an AI expert to take part, and correct that in the room.', N'Move from how the motion works to the recurring work each person will map with the discovery prompt this week.', N'If tool access is not confirmed, still run the conversation and record access blockers separately from interest, since they need different fixes. The discovery prompt runs in whichever tool the person already has. If the room stays quiet, start with your own example of work you repeat every week.', N'Close by pointing the team to the resources for next week''s topic and to the discovery prompt below. Week one is for finding the recurring work worth handing to AI. Post the usefulness question in the chat before people leave.', N'Which part of your week had you never thought of as a workflow until now? What would have to be true before you trusted AI with it?', N'Run this in Microsoft 365 Copilot, Cowork, or Scout before the next huddle, then bring the result to the session:

“I am a [your role] at Microsoft and I want to find the recurring work in my week that AI could take on.
1. First, look at my calendar, mail and Teams activity from the last four weeks and tell me what you can see about where my time actually goes. If you cannot access that data, say so and move to step 2 rather than guessing.
2. Then ask me up to five short questions, one at a time, to cover the work that never shows up in a calendar or an inbox.
3. Then list the five things I do most often. For each one give an estimate of the time it costs me per month, whether it is best handled as a saved prompt, a reusable skill, a scheduled automation or an existing agent, and the one judgment that must stay mine.
4. Rank them by time returned and tell me which one to start with.”', N'Bring the ranked list of the five things you repeat most, which of them looked like a prompt, skill, automation or agent fit, and any AI experience from the week, including anything that did not work.'),
        (N'FG-DSE-DISC', N'WF-X-DISC-01', N'DSE-DISC', N'Somebody has usually asked this customer most of your questions already. This session is about finding out what they said before you ask again.', N'In this segment a partner is frequently engaged before the engineer is, and may have run discovery of their own. The DAE owns the account relationship and the DSS covers the solution area, so three people may hold pieces of the same picture. Partner-side conversations are not in MSX and return through the Partner Timeline view or Partner Center. Facilitator note: the failure mode here is not missing information, it is asking for information somebody already has, which reads to the customer as a team that does not talk to itself.', N'How do you currently find out what has already been asked?
When did you last repeat a question the customer had already answered?
What do you need to establish yourself, whatever anyone else has done?', N'Before the session: bring one customer where you are about to start technical discovery.', N'What does the record already tell you about this customer''s estate?
Who else has spoken to them, and what would they know that is not written down?
What must you see for yourself rather than take on trust?', N'Listen for discovery restarted from scratch out of habit, for partner-held knowledge assumed to be unavailable rather than asked for, for the record treated as complete, and for engineers accepting a second-hand answer on something they will be accountable for.', N'Move from what is already known to the short list only this person can establish.', N'If nobody has upcoming discovery, use the last engagement where something important surfaced late and work out who already knew it.', N'Close by naming the one thing each person will ask a colleague or partner rather than the customer.', N'What were you about to ask a customer that somebody on your own side already knew?', N'Before the next huddle, establish what is already known about one customer''s estate from the record and from whoever else is engaged, and take only your genuinely unanswered questions to the customer.', N'Bring what you learned without asking the customer, and what you still had to ask.'),
        (N'FG-DSE-PROOF', N'WF-X-ARCH-01', N'DSE-PROOF', N'Building a bespoke proof for every customer does not survive contact with this many customers. This session is about proving the point with less.', N'Enterprise engineers can afford a tailored proof per pursuit; at this customer count that arithmetic does not work, so reuse is the constraint rather than a nice-to-have. A partner may be able to run the proof instead, which is covered separately in the design session. What matters here is deciding the smallest thing that would actually settle the customer''s doubt. Facilitator note: the most common failure is building a technically impressive proof of something the customer was never doubting.', N'How long does a proof take you, and how many can you run at once?
When did you last build something that already existed?
What was the customer actually unsure about?', N'Before the session: bring one customer asking for a demo, pilot or proof.', N'What decision is this proof supposed to settle?
Does something already exist that would settle it?
What is the smallest version that would still be convincing?', N'Listen for proofs scoped to what is interesting to build rather than to what is in doubt, for reuse dismissed without looking, for success criteria that were never agreed with the customer, and for effort that exceeds the value of the deal.', N'Move from the decision in doubt to what each person will reuse or scope.', N'If nobody has a live request, use the last proof that was built and work out what the smallest sufficient version would have been.', N'Close by naming what each person is reusing rather than building.', N'What did you build that you could have borrowed?', N'Before the next huddle, name the decision one customer''s proof must settle, look for something that already exists before building anything, and scope the smallest sufficient version.', N'Bring what you found already existing, and what you still had to build.'),
        (N'FG-DSE-CONV', N'WF-X-CONV-01', N'DSE-CONV', N'Most technical questions reach you second-hand, and your answer goes back the same way. This session is about writing for the relay.', N'At this scale the engineer is frequently not in the room. A partner or a seller carries the question in and the answer back, which means the question has already been through one translation before you see it and the answer goes through another on the way out. CELA rules govern what may be exchanged with a partner: personal data, MSX screenshots and exports, and financial or seat-level detail are off-limits. Facilitator note: an answer that requires your presence to make sense is not finished, because you will not be there.', N'How often are you answering a question the customer asked somebody else?
When has an answer of yours arrived at a customer distorted?
What do you put in writing differently when a partner will forward it?', N'Before the session: bring one technical question that reached you through somebody else.', N'What do you think the customer actually asked, as opposed to what reached you?
Which part of your answer would you least like to see paraphrased?
What would this look like if the person relaying it did not understand it?', N'Listen for the relayed question taken at face value, for answers written for a technical peer rather than for the relayer, for caveats that will not survive being summarized, and for detail that CELA rules exclude from partner exchange.', N'Move from the reconstructed question to the answer each person will write for relay.', N'If nobody has a live question, use one that came back a second time and work out what was lost in the first answer.', N'Close by naming the part of each answer that must not be paraphrased.', N'Which of your answers has been repeated to a customer in a form you would not recognize?', N'Before the next huddle, answer one relayed technical question in a form that survives being forwarded, and mark explicitly what must be passed on word for word.', N'Bring the answer you wrote and what came back.'),
        (N'FG-DSE-HEALTH', N'WF-X-HEALTH-01', N'DSE-HEALTH', N'You will not notice a customer''s technical problem by looking at them, because you are not looking at most of them and you do not operate any of it. You notice by recognizing a pattern you have already met.', N'The technical estate here is operated by partners and customers rather than by us, and there are far more customers per engineer than in Enterprise, so risk is found by pattern rather than by inspection. The problems worth tracking are the ones you have already diagnosed once: a configuration that fails under load, a version combination that breaks on upgrade, an integration that degrades quietly. Consumption movement belongs to the D-CSA and is not the engineer''s signal. The intervention normally runs through whoever operates the solution. Facilitator note: the judgment here is about noticing and routing rather than fixing.', N'How would you currently find out a customer''s solution was in trouble?
Which technical problem have you now seen at more than one customer?
Who actually fixes it when you find one?', N'Before the session: bring one technical problem you have seen at more than one customer.', N'Which of your customers are running the configuration you already know is fragile?
Which of them has a partner who probably knows already?
What can you actually do about it from where you sit?', N'Listen for problems treated as one-offs when they have already recurred, for partner-operated estates assumed to be unmonitored, for engineers taking on a fix that belongs to whoever operates it, and for warnings sent without a route to action.', N'Move from the exposed list to the one fix each person routes.', N'If nobody has a recurring problem, use the last customer escalation and ask whether anything about it was predictable.', N'Close by naming the customer each person is warning and who is actually going to fix it.', N'Which problem had you already seen twice without treating it as a pattern?', N'Before the next huddle, identify which of your customers are exposed to a technical problem you have already met elsewhere, and route one fix to whoever operates the solution.', N'Bring the pattern, who was exposed, and where you routed it.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-ORIENT', N'WF-ORIENT-01', N'DCSA-ORIENT', N'This is the first of eight huddles, and the one with no AI activity in it. Today is about what the motion is and what it asks of you. Frontier Accelerator exists because we are asking customers to reshape how they work around AI, and that argument is stronger when we have done it ourselves first. That is what Customer Zero means here: we apply these tools to our own real work, and we are straight about where they help and where they do not.', N'Weekly and short, led by the manager with a facilitator. The cadence carries the motion, so if the slot has to move, move it rather than drop it.
Four principles hold every week: keep the weekly rhythm, learn from what colleagues have actually tried, ground every session in real work rather than hypothetical scenarios, and try something between huddles so there is an experience to bring back.
Every huddle from next week runs to the same shape, and the agenda and timings are set out on the huddle page: a short framing of why the topic matters, then sharing what you tried since last time, then hands-on work on a real scenario, then a close where you pick the one thing you will carry into the week.
Five roles make the motion work. The Leader connects it to strategy and clears barriers. The Manager owns the huddle and opens with the why. The Facilitator runs it and draws every voice in. The Team Member experiments in their own workflow and brings back wins and friction. The Coach reviews patterns across huddles and feeds insight back.
The journey ahead: this week you get grounded, weeks two to eight you build the habit on real work, week nine the team decides which habits stick and what comes next, and from week ten you choose your own topics.
You are here as a peer, not an expert. You do not need prior AI experience, and “I do not know” is a good answer.
Two things on feedback. The facilitator posts one question in the chat each week, asking how useful the huddle was on a scale of one to ten. Huddle transcripts are reviewed for themes to improve the sessions, so keep confidential detail out of the room, whether that is customer, partner, enrollment or commercial, and put product problems into the tool''s own feedback control rather than here.', N'Which part of your week is assembling information rather than deciding anything?
Where do you already use AI, and where have you deliberately avoided it?
What would you need to see before you trusted an AI-drafted output in front of a customer?
What would make thirty minutes a week worth protecting for this?', N'Before the session: complete the baseline survey if it has been sent to you, confirm the huddle series is on your calendar, confirm you can open at least one of the tools named for your path, since the commitment at the end of this session runs in one of them, and come ready to describe the work you repeat most weeks. If your access is blocked, come anyway and say so, because that is a different problem from not being interested. No prior AI experience is needed for this session.', N'Which work do you repeat every week without ever thinking of it as a workflow?
Which of those steps is assembling information rather than making a decision?
Where does your judgment have to stay human no matter how good the output gets?
What would have to happen in these thirty minutes for you to want the next one?', N'Listen for unvoiced skepticism, for access blockers stated as disinterest, and for the assumption that this is a tool rollout. Listen for people describing only their exceptional work rather than the work they repeat, because the recurring and unglamorous tasks are the ones worth mapping. Listen for anyone who thinks they need to be an AI expert to take part, and correct that in the room.', N'Move from how the motion works to the recurring work each person will map with the discovery prompt this week.', N'If tool access is not confirmed, still run the conversation and record access blockers separately from interest, since they need different fixes. The discovery prompt runs in whichever tool the person already has. If the room stays quiet, start with your own example of work you repeat every week.', N'Close by pointing the team to the resources for next week''s topic and to the discovery prompt below. Week one is for finding the recurring work worth handing to AI. Post the usefulness question in the chat before people leave.', N'Which part of your week had you never thought of as a workflow until now? What would have to be true before you trusted AI with it?', N'Run this in Microsoft 365 Copilot, Cowork, or Scout before the next huddle, then bring the result to the session:

“I am a [your role] at Microsoft and I want to find the recurring work in my week that AI could take on.
1. First, look at my calendar, mail and Teams activity from the last four weeks and tell me what you can see about where my time actually goes. If you cannot access that data, say so and move to step 2 rather than guessing.
2. Then ask me up to five short questions, one at a time, to cover the work that never shows up in a calendar or an inbox.
3. Then list the five things I do most often. For each one give an estimate of the time it costs me per month, whether it is best handled as a saved prompt, a reusable skill, a scheduled automation or an existing agent, and the one judgment that must stay mine.
4. Rank them by time returned and tell me which one to start with.”', N'Bring the ranked list of the five things you repeat most, which of them looked like a prompt, skill, automation or agent fit, and any AI experience from the week, including anything that did not work.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-PARTNER', N'WF-X-PARTNER-01', N'DCSA-PARTNER', N'On these accounts the technical work is done by a partner you have no authority over, with no support agreement behind you. This session is about making a handover that actually gets acted on.', N'Consumption execution in this segment runs through the D-CSA and partner delivery, particularly on non-Unified Azure. Unlike a Unified engagement there is no funded delivery vehicle and no contractual escalation route, so the handover has to stand on its own. Partner Center sits outside what our agents can reach, so partner-side progress returns through the Partner Timeline view or Partner Center directly. CELA rules govern the exchange: personal data, MSX screenshots and exports, and financial or seat-level detail are off-limits. Facilitator note: the useful frame is that you are writing for a competent engineer who was not in any of your conversations.', N'When you hand technical work to a partner, how do you find out whether it happened?
What do you end up doing yourself because handing it over felt slower?
What does a partner need from you that you usually leave out?', N'Before the session: bring one account where a partner is doing or should be doing the technical work.', N'What would this partner need to act on this without calling you?
How would you know in a month whether anything happened?
What are you holding onto that you should have handed over?', N'Listen for handovers that assume context the partner does not have, for work retained because delegating felt slower, for progress assumed from silence, and for detail being prepared that CELA rules exclude.', N'Move from the handover to the signal each person will use to check it landed.', N'If nobody has a live handover, use the last one that stalled and work out what was missing from it.', N'Close by naming what each person is handing over and the signal they will check.', N'What did you keep because explaining it felt like more work than doing it?', N'Before the next huddle, hand one piece of technical work to a partner in a form they can act on unaided, and write down the specific signal you will check to know whether it worked.', N'Bring what you handed over and what the signal showed.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-CONV', N'WF-X-CONV-01', N'DCSA-CONV', N'You are probably not coming back to this account. This session is about preparing for a conversation on that assumption.', N'A Unified engagement supports an ongoing advisory relationship with recurring contact. At this coverage ratio and without that agreement, most customers get a single technical conversation and then nothing until something breaks. That changes the objective from building understanding over time to making one intervention that survives your absence. Facilitator note: the temptation is to cover everything because you may not be back, and that reliably produces a conversation the customer remembers nothing from.', N'How many of your accounts have you spoken to more than once?
What happens at an account after you leave it?
When did you last try to cover everything in one meeting?', N'Before the session: bring one customer you have a technical conversation coming up with.', N'What is the single thing this customer most needs to get right?
Who will be here after you are not?
What would still be true in six months if you did nothing else?', N'Listen for agendas that try to cover everything, for advice that depends on the architect returning, for no named owner on the customer or partner side, and for durable guidance never written down.', N'Move from the single priority to what each person will leave behind.', N'If nobody has an upcoming conversation, use an account they have not revisited and ask what is still standing from the last one.', N'Close by naming the one thing each person is leaving behind and who is picking it up.', N'Which of your advice quietly assumed you would be coming back?', N'Before the next huddle, prepare one customer conversation around a single priority, and leave something written that a customer or partner could act on without you.', N'Bring what you left behind and who owns it now.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-VALUE', N'WF-X-VALUE-01', N'DCSA-VALUE', N'Somebody else delivered most of what worked at your accounts. This session is about what you can honestly say about it.', N'A Unified engagement leaves a delivery record - milestones, hours, an engagement manager - that can be pointed at. On non-Unified accounts none of that exists, so the evidence is telemetry plus whatever the partner is willing to share. Separate what was deployed from what is being used and from what the customer got value from; those are three different claims and only the first is reliably in the record. The renewal conversation belongs to the DAE and the CE, so evidence that stays with you does not reach it. Facilitator note: partner-delivered outcomes are the partner''s to describe, and claiming them damages the relationship that is doing the work.', N'When an account goes well, where is the proof?
How much of what worked was delivered by a partner?
Who asks you for value evidence, and when?', N'Before the session: bring two or three accounts where you believe the technical outcome was good.', N'What can you evidence about that outcome as opposed to believe?
Whose outcome is it to describe?
Who needs this before the renewal, and have they got it?', N'Listen for deployment presented as value, for partner-delivered outcomes claimed as ours, for evidence that exists only in the architect''s head, and for renewals approaching with nobody having asked.', N'Move from the evidence review to what each person passes on and to whom.', N'If nobody has a clear success, use an account that renewed quietly and ask what evidence would have been available if anyone had wanted it.', N'Close by naming what each person is passing on and who is receiving it.', N'Which good outcome would have gone unrecorded if nobody asked?', N'Before the next huddle, establish what one account''s technical outcome can actually be evidenced with, separate what the partner delivered, and send it to whoever owns the renewal.', N'Bring what you could evidence, what you could not, and who you sent it to.'),
        (N'FG-PSS-ORIENT', N'WF-ORIENT-01', N'PSS-ORIENT', N'This is the first of eight huddles, and the one with no AI activity in it. Today is about what the motion is and what it asks of you. Frontier Accelerator exists because we are asking customers to reshape how they work around AI, and that argument is stronger when we have done it ourselves first. That is what Customer Zero means here: we apply these tools to our own real work, and we are straight about where they help and where they do not.', N'Weekly and short, led by the manager with a facilitator. The cadence carries the motion, so if the slot has to move, move it rather than drop it.
Four principles hold every week: keep the weekly rhythm, learn from what colleagues have actually tried, ground every session in real work rather than hypothetical scenarios, and try something between huddles so there is an experience to bring back.
