        (N'FG-PSS-DEMAND', N'WF-X-OPP-01', N'PSS-DEMAND', N'Most roles here decide what to send out to a partner. You also have to decide what to do with what comes back the other way.', N'Partner-originated demand arrives qualified to the partner''s standard rather than ours, and the windows are already running: a partner has fourteen days to accept a referral before it expires, and sixty days to convert it before the system marks it lost. IP co-sell registration has its own conditions - the deal must be won on a Microsoft-managed account, be Azure IP co-sell eligible, be at least twenty-five thousand US dollars, and have a seventy-two hour gap between creation and being marked won. CELA rules limit what can be exchanged in either direction. Facilitator note: the honest difficulty here is that you often cannot verify what the partner is telling you, and deciding how much to invest on their word is the actual skill.', N'How much of your pipeline originates with a partner rather than with us?
How do you decide whether to believe a partner''s qualification?
When did a partner-sourced deal turn out not to be real?', N'Before the session: bring one opportunity that came to you from a partner.', N'What do you actually know about this opportunity as opposed to what you were told?
What would you need to see to invest Microsoft resource behind it?
Which of your open partner-sourced items are closest to a window closing?', N'Listen for partner qualification accepted at face value, for registration conditions assumed rather than checked, for windows running unnoticed, and for reluctance to push back on a partner in case it damages the relationship.', N'Move from the verification read to the one opportunity each person progresses or declines.', N'If nobody has a live partner-sourced opportunity, use the last one that went nowhere and work out what would have revealed it earlier.', N'Close by naming the opportunity each person is progressing and the one they are declining.', N'Which partner-sourced opportunity have you been carrying on trust?', N'Before the next huddle, establish what can actually be verified about one partner-originated opportunity, check its registration conditions, and either progress it or tell the partner why you are not.', N'Bring what you could verify, what you could not, and what you decided.'),
        (N'FG-PSS-ORCH', N'WF-X-ORCH-01', N'PSS-ORCH', N'A cadence commitment across a portfolio of partners becomes a diary full of status calls unless somebody decides what each one is for. This session is about that decision.', N'The partner rhythm in this segment includes a weekly partner meeting with the account roles and a minimum of monthly contact per partner seller, alongside deal clinics and the bi-weekly territory health review. Meeting a minimum is not the same as running a portfolio, and the partners who most need attention are rarely the ones who ask for it. Facilitator note: the useful distinction is between a meeting that needs an agenda because something has to be decided, and one that needs only a check-in because nothing has changed - treating them the same is what fills the week.', N'How many partner meetings do you hold in a month, and how many changed anything?
Which partner have you not spoken to in longer than you should admit?
What makes a partner meeting worth having?', N'Before the session: bring your partner list and roughly when you last spoke to each.', N'Which of your partners has something that actually needs deciding this month?
Which are you meeting out of obligation?
Who is quietly getting no attention at all?', N'Listen for cadence treated as the objective rather than the floor, for the loudest partners absorbing the time, for meetings with no decision attached, and for the quiet partners going unmentioned.', N'Move from the triage to the agenda each person will set for their one substantive meeting.', N'If someone has few partners, work the last month of meetings and ask which produced a decision.', N'Close by naming the partner each person is giving real time to and the ones they are handling lightly.', N'Which partner has been getting your time because they ask for it rather than because they need it?', N'Before the next huddle, triage three to five of your partners for the month, set a real agenda for the one meeting that needs a decision, and make sure your monthly contact commitment is met across the partner sellers you carry.', N'Bring your triage and what the substantive meeting decided.'),
        (N'FG-PSS-VALUE', N'WF-X-VALUE-01', N'PSS-VALUE', N'When a partner closes and delivers, Microsoft largely stops seeing the customer. You are the only route back.', N'In CSP the partner frequently leads the later stages and often closes, which means our record ends roughly where the customer''s experience begins. The PSS has visibility into the partner relationship without owning execution, so the realistic goal is closing an information gap rather than driving an outcome. What the partner will tell you is governed by the CELA rules in both directions, and a partner is under no obligation to report customer outcomes to us at all. Facilitator note: the value of asking is as much about the partner relationship as about the answer - a partner who is asked how the customer is doing behaves differently from one who is only asked about pipeline.', N'What do you know about customers your partners closed last quarter?
Who would tell you if one of them was unhappy?
When did you last ask a partner about a customer rather than about a deal?', N'Before the session: bring two or three deals a partner closed in the last few quarters.', N'What does our record actually show about these customers since the close?
What would you want to know that only the partner can tell you?
What is in it for the partner to tell you?', N'Listen for usage data mistaken for customer satisfaction, for the assumption that silence means success, for questions framed as inspection rather than as interest, and for renewal dates approaching with nobody having asked anything.', N'Move from the visibility gap to the one question each person will put to a partner.', N'If nobody has recent closed deals, use a renewal that was lost and ask when the first sign was available.', N'Close by naming the partner each person is asking and the question they are asking.', N'Which customer do you know nothing about since the day it closed?', N'Before the next huddle, map what our record can and cannot tell you about customers a partner closed, and ask one partner directly how a specific customer is getting on.', N'Bring what you could not see, what the partner told you, and whether it surprised you.'),
        (N'FG-SMEC-CE-POLICY', N'WF-X-POLICY-01', N'SMEC-CE-POLICY', N'Every deal meets the policy questions eventually. This session is about meeting them while the structure is still a question rather than a commitment.', N'Policy, licensing and eligibility interpretation always routes to the authoritative owner rather than to an agent or to your own reading. In this segment a substantial share transacts through CSP, which carries its own eligibility conditions - Modern Commerce and an accepted agreement with a pre-existing partner billing relationship, with legacy arrangements excluded - so the route matters as much as the structure. Facilitator note: the failure mode is not getting a policy answer wrong, it is answering at all when the question belonged to somebody else, because an answer from the commercial person is treated as authoritative.', N'When did a policy question last surface too late to change anything?
Which questions do you find yourself answering that you should be routing?
How long does an authoritative answer actually take to get?', N'Before the session: bring one live deal where the commercial structure is not yet fixed.', N'What in this deal has an eligibility or licensing condition attached to it?
Which of those can you answer from approved guidance, and which need the owner?
What would it cost to find out at approval instead of now?', N'Listen for policy answered from memory or from a previous deal, for eligibility assumed because a similar customer qualified, for CSP conditions treated as the same as direct, and for questions deferred because getting an answer feels slow.', N'Move from the question list to the one each person will route today.', N'If nobody has a live deal, use the last one where a policy issue surfaced late and work out when it first became answerable.', N'Close by naming the question each person is routing and who they are routing it to.', N'Which policy question have you been answering yourself?', N'Before the next huddle, run one live deal against the policy and eligibility questions it will face, and route every unresolved one to its authoritative owner rather than carrying it.', N'Bring the question you routed and what the answer changed.'),
        (N'FG-SMEC-CE-NEG', N'WF-X-DEAL-01', N'SMEC-CE-NEG', N'In this segment the negotiation is frequently conducted by a partner. Your concession logic has to work when applied by somebody who does not report to you.', N'The Commercial Executive owns concession authority and the commercial boundary, but in a partner-transacted deal the conversation with the customer is often the partner''s. That changes the artefact from a negotiation plan you will execute into a boundary somebody else has to hold. CELA rules govern what can be shared with the partner: personal data, screenshots and exports, and financial or seat-level detail are off-limits. Facilitator note: the useful question in the room is what happens to your boundary when you are not there to defend it.', N'On your last partner-transacted deal, who actually did the negotiating?
What did you concede that you had not planned to?
How do you tell a partner what they cannot offer without telling them your floor?', N'Before the session: bring one live deal heading toward a negotiation.', N'What can be conceded on this deal without approval, and what cannot?
How does the partner know where that line is?
What would trigger you to escalate rather than concede?', N'Listen for concession authority assumed rather than confirmed, for boundaries that exist only in the CE''s head, for partner briefings that reveal the floor, and for escalation triggers defined after the pressure has started.', N'Move from the boundary to how each person will communicate it to the partner.', N'If nobody has a live negotiation, use one that conceded more than intended and work out where the boundary failed.', N'Close by naming the boundary each person is setting and how the partner will learn it.', N'Where has a concession been made in your name that you would not have made?', N'Before the next huddle, write down the concession logic and authority limits for one live deal, name the escalation trigger, and brief the partner on what cannot be conceded without revealing your floor.', N'Bring what you told the partner and whether the boundary held.'),
        (N'FG-SMEC-CE-ORCH', N'WF-X-ORCH-01', N'SMEC-CE-ORCH', N'Approvals discovered one at a time cost a week each. This session is about finding them all at once, including the ones that are not yours to obtain.', N'On a partner-transacted deal the approval path runs across two organizations, and the steps on the partner side are visible only through the Partner Timeline view or Partner Center rather than through our own systems. Approval lead times drift with quarter load, so a plan built on last quarter''s turnaround will miss. Facilitator note: the distinction worth drawing is between an approval you can obtain, one you can only request, and one the partner has to obtain from their own side - all three look the same on a plan and behave completely differently.', N'How do you currently find out what approvals a structure will need?
Which approvals on your last deal were discovered late?
What happens when the partner needs an approval from their own organization?', N'Before the session: bring one deal with a commercial structure that needs approving.', N'Which approvals does this structure trigger, and who owns each?
Which are genuinely sequential and which have you been treating as sequential out of habit?
What does the partner have to obtain that you cannot?', N'Listen for approval owners named by role rather than by person, for parallel approvals treated as a chain, for partner-side steps assumed to be quick, and for evidence requirements discovered at submission.', N'Move from the mapped path to the one approval each person will start today.
Customer Zero cue - use only if it happens: when someone reverts, rejects or overrides what the agent produced, stop and name it. Ask what the agent could not see. Huddle transcripts are reviewed for themes, so what is said here does reach us - keep customer-confidential detail out of it.', N'If nobody has a live deal, reconstruct the approval path of the last one that slipped and identify where the time went.', N'Close by naming the approval each person is starting and what evidence it needs.', N'Which approval did you assume somebody else was handling?', N'Before the next huddle, map the approval path for one deal with a named owner and the required evidence for each step, mark which belong to the partner, and start the one on the critical path.', N'Bring the map and what you found was missing from it.'),
        (N'FG-SMEC-CE-DEAL2', N'WF-X-DEAL-01', N'SMEC-CE-DEAL2', N'A commercial structure that cannot be explained without you in the room will not get approved when you are not. This session is about making one reviewable.', N'In this segment the CE is frequently absent from the conversation where the structure is actually decided - the partner is in the room and the approver reads a document. That makes the written case the product, not a summary of it. Separate the three things an approver needs: what decision they are being asked to make, whether the numbers can be checked, and what is being asserted without support. Facilitator note: the instinct is to make the case persuasive. Push the group towards making it inspectable instead, because an approver who cannot verify a figure will not approve around it.', N'When did an approval last stall on something other than the merits?
What in your last commercial case could the approver not have checked?
Who has to be able to read this when you are not there?', N'Before the session: bring one commercial structure you have agreed and still need to take to approval.', N'What exactly is the approver being asked to decide?
Which figure in this could someone else not verify?
What have you asserted that you have not shown?', N'Listen for cases that argue rather than evidence, for figures whose source nobody can name, for judgments stated as facts, and for people describing what they would say in the room when they will not be in the room.', N'Move from the narrative to the one gap each person will close before sending it.', N'If nobody has a live structure awaiting approval, use the last one that was approved and ask what the approver had to take on trust.', N'Close by naming the single unevidenced claim each person is going to close before their case goes out.', N'What in your last approval request did the approver have to take on trust?', N'Before the next huddle, write the decision narrative for one commercial structure, have it read back for unsupported claims, and close the gap on the central one.', N'Bring the ask as you stated it, the gap that came back, and what you changed.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-ARCH', N'WF-X-ARCH-01', N'DCSA-ARCH', N'Architecture in this segment is judged by whether it still works six months after you have moved on. This session is about designing for that.', N'A DCSA carries a portfolio of non-unified customers - no dedicated support relationship, often no in-house platform team, and frequently a partner doing the implementation. That changes what a good design is. The elegant option that assumes someone will tune it is worse than the plain option that runs untouched. Separate the design decisions that need your expertise from the ones the customer or partner will end up making anyway, and make the second group hard to get wrong. Facilitator note: the instinct is to compare architectures on capability. Push the group to compare them on what happens when nobody is watching.', N'Who is actually going to build this?
What does this design assume somebody will maintain?
What breaks first if nobody touches it for six months?', N'Before the session: bring one architecture you are designing or reviewing now, and know who will implement it.', N'What does this design assume about the people running it?
Which decision here did you make because it was right, and which because it was familiar?
What would you simplify if you knew nobody would ever tune it?', N'Listen for designs justified by capability rather than by operability, for assumptions about customer skill that nobody has tested, for a partner implementer who has not been consulted, and for support models being assumed rather than confirmed.', N'Move from the design to the single assumption each person will go and verify.', N'If nobody has a live design, take an architecture already deployed on one of their accounts and ask what it assumed that turned out not to be true.', N'Close by naming the one operability assumption each person will confirm with the implementer this week.', N'Which of your designs needed you more than it should have?', N'Before the next huddle, take one design to the person who will build it and confirm they can operate it without you.', N'Bring the assumption you tested and what the implementer said.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-HEALTH', N'WF-X-HEALTH-01', N'DCSA-HEALTH', N'Every consumption problem has a technical cause that appeared first. This session is about catching it at that point rather than at the review.', N'Across a portfolio of this size nobody can inspect accounts individually, so technical risk is found either by a signal or by a complaint. The gap between those two is where the DCSA earns their place. Distinguish three things that look identical from outside: a workload that was never fully deployed, one that was deployed and abandoned, and one that is working but small. Only the first two are yours. Facilitator note: this session pairs with a standing check - the point is not the one-off scan but the rhythm that replaces it.', N'When did you last find a technical problem before the customer did?
What told you about your last consumption drop, and how late was it?
Which of your accounts could be in trouble without you knowing?', N'Before the session: know which consumption or usage view you can actually reach for your portfolio.', N'What is the earliest signal you could realistically have seen?
Which risks here would the customer dispute?
What would you want to be told automatically every week?', N'Listen for risk lists with no owner, for signals being read as problems without a technical hypothesis, for people describing manual checks they know they will not sustain, and for accounts nobody has looked at in months being called healthy.', N'Move from the scan to the standing check that means they do not have to remember to run it.', N'If consumption data is not reachable, use support and service records instead and ask what those alone can and cannot tell you.', N'Close by having each person name the risk they are acting on and confirm their standing check is running.', N'What did you find late that you could have found early?', N'Before the next huddle, start one technical intervention and leave a standing check running on your portfolio.', N'Bring the risk you acted on, what the check has surfaced since, and whether any of it was new.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-EXPAND', N'WF-X-RENEW-01', N'DCSA-EXPAND', N'Expansion in this segment is found in the telemetry of what is already working, not built in a deck. This session is about reading that evidence.', N'A DCSA is accountable for uncovering the next logical workload, but not for selling it - that boundary matters and gets blurred under pressure. Readiness has three parts and all three have to hold: the customer is succeeding with something adjacent, they have the technical capacity to take on more, and somebody on their side wants it. Two out of three produces a stalled deployment that damages the account you were trying to grow. Facilitator note: watch for expansion proposed from what Microsoft wants to sell rather than from what the customer has proven they can run.', N'Which of your accounts is genuinely succeeding with something right now?
What did your last expansion attempt assume that was not true?
Who on the customer side would actually want the next workload?', N'Before the session: bring one account where a workload is deployed and working.', N'What is the evidence this customer is ready rather than just suitable?
Who asked for this, or did we?
What would have to be true technically before this could start?', N'Listen for expansion built on product fit rather than customer readiness, for deployed being read as adopted, for no named customer sponsor, and for DCSAs carrying the commercial conversation themselves rather than handing it over.', N'Move from the hypothesis to who owns the conversation and what they need from it.', N'If nobody has a succeeding workload, work the opposite way: take a stalled deployment and ask what would have had to be true for it to have worked.', N'Close by naming the owner of each expansion conversation and what evidence goes with it.', N'Which expansion did you propose that the customer was not ready for?', N'Before the next huddle, evidence one expansion hypothesis and hand it to whoever owns the commercial conversation.', N'Bring the readiness evidence, who you handed it to, and what they did with it.'),
        (N'FG-DSE-ASSET', N'WF-X-ASSET-01', N'DSE-ASSET', N'The same technical problem arrives at this team repeatedly wearing different customer names. This session is about answering it once.', N'Reuse fails for a predictable reason: the asset gets saved and the context does not. Another engineer finds a script or a design and cannot tell what it assumed, so they rebuild it, and now there are two. The reusable unit is the artefact plus its constraints - what it was built for, what it depends on, and where it does not apply. Sanitization matters more here than in Enterprise because at this volume an asset gets reused many times before anyone reviews it. Facilitator note: the community hubs already hold field-contributed assets; check before building.', N'What have you built more than once?
What have you looked for and failed to find?
What would somebody need to know before reusing your last piece of work?', N'Before the session: bring one artefact you built recently that you think somebody else could use.', N'Could somebody use this without talking to you?
What does it assume that is not written down?
Where would this be wrong to apply?', N'Listen for assets shared without their constraints, for customer-identifying detail left in, for reuse being treated as filing rather than as writing, and for people describing things they rebuilt that already existed somewhere.', N'Move from the artefact to where it will live and who will find it.
Customer Zero cue - use only if it happens: when someone reverts, rejects or overrides what the agent produced, stop and name it. Ask what the agent could not see. Huddle transcripts are reviewed for themes, so what is said here does reach us - keep customer-confidential detail out of it.', N'If nobody has an artefact, use the last question they answered twice and capture the answer instead.', N'Close by confirming where each asset is going and who else has been told it exists.', N'What did you rebuild this quarter that already existed?', N'Before the next huddle, publish one asset with its limitations stated, and tell the team where it is.', N'Bring the asset, where it now lives, and whether anyone has used it.'),
        (N'FG-DSE-PARTNER', N'WF-X-ORCH-01', N'DSE-PARTNER', N'When a partner-led deal stops moving, the cause is usually that somebody on the partner side is not confident enough to take the next technical step. This session is about finding that and fixing it properly.', N'This is the one part of the DSE path with no Enterprise equivalent, and it carries a genuine tension: the fastest way to unblock a partner-led deal is to do the technical work yourself, and that is exactly what prevents the partner ever becoming self-sufficient. The distinction to hold is between a gap in knowledge, which enablement fixes, and a gap in capacity, which it does not. Facilitator note: partner-side detail is limited in our systems, so most of the evidence here comes from what the partner tells you and how their deals actually behave.', N'Which of your partner-led deals keep needing you?
What do you end up explaining more than once?
Is the partner missing knowledge, or missing people?', N'Before the session: bring one partner-led opportunity that has stalled or that keeps returning to you.', N'What exactly can the partner not do yet?
Is that knowledge, capacity, or willingness?
What would let them do this without you next time?', N'Listen for DSEs absorbing partner work and calling it enablement, for capability gaps described as attitude problems, for enablement with no owner or date, and for the same partner appearing behind several stalled deals.', N'Move from the gap to the specific enablement action and who owns it on the partner side.', N'If nobody has a stalled partner deal, use the last one they unblocked and ask what would have happened if they had been unavailable.', N'Close by naming the enablement action, its owner on the partner side, and the date.', N'What are you doing for a partner that they should be able to do themselves?', N'Before the next huddle, name one partner technical gap and agree an enablement action with a named owner and a date.', N'Bring the gap, the action agreed, and whether the partner did it.'),
        (N'FG-DSS-COMP', N'WF-X-DEAL-01', N'DSS-COMP', N'Competitive positions usually fail because they answer a comparison the customer was not making. This session is about finding the real one.', N'A DSS carries a solution area across many accounts, so the same competitive conversation recurs with small variations - which makes it worth getting right once. Three things get confused: what we do better, what the customer values, and what they are actually comparing us to. Only the overlap of all three is a differentiator. Being honest about where a competitor is stronger is what makes the rest of the position credible, and sellers consistently underrate how obvious the omission is to a customer who has already seen the other vendor. Facilitator note: push for the specific competitor and the specific criteria, not a general positioning discussion.', N'What is this customer actually comparing us to?
Which of our advantages have they told you they care about?
Where is the competitor genuinely better?', N'Before the session: bring one live opportunity where a competitor is involved and you know who it is.', N'What are the customer''s own decision criteria, in their words?
Which differentiator survives contact with those criteria?
What will you concede, and when?', N'Listen for feature comparisons standing in for customer criteria, for competitor weaknesses asserted without a source, for positions that concede nothing, and for the same objection appearing across several accounts without anyone having prepared for it.', N'Move from the position to the one objection each person will prepare properly.', N'If nobody has a live competitive deal, take the objection they hear most often across the solution area and build the position around that instead.', N'Close by naming the objection each person will prepare and the evidence they will bring to it.', N'Which competitive claim have you made that you could not evidence?', N'Before the next huddle, test one competitive position with a customer and record which part of it actually landed.', N'Bring the position, what the customer responded to, and what you dropped.'),
        (N'FG-DSS-ORCH', N'WF-X-ORCH-01', N'DSS-ORCH', N'In this segment nobody is assigned to your opportunity. Everything you need comes from someone who is also doing their own job. This session is about asking well.', N'An ask that describes a situation gets sympathy; an ask that describes a specific task with a date gets done. The three things that make an ask answerable are what exactly is needed, by when, and what happens if it does not arrive - the last one is almost always omitted and is what moves an ask up someone''s list. Separate asks that need a person from asks that need a decision, because they go to different places and have different lead times. Facilitator note: watch for people who have absorbed a task themselves rather than asking, and ask what that cost them.', N'What are you doing yourself that you should have asked for?
What did you last ask for that never arrived?
Would the person receiving your ask know exactly what to do?', N'Before the session: bring one opportunity where you need support you have not yet asked for.', N'What exactly do you need, and by when?
Who specifically, not which team?
What happens to the deal if it does not come?', N'Listen for asks addressed to a team rather than a person, for needs described as context rather than as tasks, for no date, and for people who have stopped asking because previous asks went unanswered.', N'Move from the ask to the standing check that tells them when it has gone quiet.', N'If nobody has a live need, use the last support ask that went unanswered and rewrite it as it should have been sent.', N'Close by confirming each ask has a person, a task and a date, and that a check is running.', N'What did you stop asking for because it never came?', N'Before the next huddle, send one specific support ask and set a check that tells you if it goes unanswered.', N'Bring the ask, whether it was answered, and how long it took.'),
        (N'FG-PSS-CAPACITY', N'WF-X-HEALTH-01', N'PSS-CAPACITY', N'Partner capability and partner capacity look identical from the outside and fail in completely different ways. This session is about telling them apart before you route work.', N'Capability is whether they know how; capacity is whether they have anyone free. Enablement fixes the first and does nothing for the second, which is why so much partner enablement is politely attended and never applied. The evidence available to us is partial - program participation and past delivery tell you about capability, and almost nothing tells you about capacity, which usually has to be asked. Facilitator note: a partner rarely says no to work, so absence of refusal is not evidence of readiness.', N'Which of your partners have you routed work to without checking?
When a partner-led engagement last went wrong, was it skill or bandwidth?
How would you know a partner was overcommitted?', N'Before the session: bring one partner you are currently routing work to or considering.', N'Is this a knowledge gap or a people gap?
What evidence do you have, and what are you assuming?
What would you have to see to route confidently?', N'Listen for capability judged from the partner''s own claims, for capacity never being asked about, for program certifications treated as delivery evidence, and for partners being chosen by relationship rather than by readiness.', N'Move from the assessment to the specific action and who on the partner side owns it.', N'If nobody has a live routing decision, use a partner engagement that went badly and work out which of the two gaps it actually was.', N'Close by naming the gap, the closing action, its partner-side owner and the date.', N'Which partner have you kept using out of habit rather than evidence?', N'Before the next huddle, assess one partner''s readiness properly and agree one closing action with them.', N'Bring the gap you found, what you agreed, and whether the partner acted.'),
        (N'FG-PSS-CONV', N'WF-X-CONV-01', N'PSS-CONV', N'Partners agree to a great deal in meetings and act on very little of it. The difference is almost always whether the ask made sense in their economics.', N'You influence without authority across more partners than you can meet, so each conversation has to carry weight. A partner''s economics are not a mystery - margin, utilization of their consultants, recurring revenue and how quickly they get paid - and framing an ask against any of those changes the response. The most common failure is a conversation that reports Microsoft''s view of the partnership back to a partner who experiences it completely differently. Facilitator note: Know Your Partner is purpose-built for assembling this briefing; confirm access before relying on it.', N'What does this partner actually make money on?
What did you last ask a partner to do, and did they?
What would they say the partnership is currently worth to them?', N'Before the session: bring one partner you have a conversation coming up with.', N'What is in this for their business specifically?
What is your one ask?
What will they raise that you have not prepared for?', N'Listen for asks framed in Microsoft targets, for partner economics being assumed rather than known, for several asks where there should be one, and for briefings that report performance without proposing anything.', N'Move from the briefing to rehearsing the one ask and the objection they expect.', N'If Know Your Partner is not available to you, assemble the same evidence from partner performance reporting and use the rest of the workflow unchanged.', N'Close by confirming each person has one ask, framed in partner economics, and has rehearsed the objection.', N'When did a partner agree with you and then not act?', N'Before the next huddle, have one partner conversation with a single ask framed in their economics, and record what they committed to.', N'Bring the ask, what they committed to, and whether it happened.'),
        (N'FG-DSS-INVEST', N'WF-INVEST-01', N'DSS-INVEST', N'Investment in SME&C is not one thing. This session is about picking the right route before you build anything.', N'Investment in this segment is a selection problem before it is a submission problem. There are several routes - direct customer investment, partner-led programs, and voucher-based offers - and most sellers learn one and use it for everything. The FY27 SME&C customer investment analysis records that Azure post-sales investment expects first-year ACR above roughly 500,000 US dollars, and that below that threshold the motion routes partner-led; it also records vouchers as the scale vehicle, at around 20,000 US dollars for Corporate against 50,000 for Enterprise. Treat those as orientation from that analysis rather than as current policy - confirm any figure with the authoritative owner before it reaches a customer. The point of the session is that choosing the wrong route wastes weeks that the right route would not have cost. For a DSS the relevant question is usually whether the solution-area opportunity is large enough to carry direct investment or whether it belongs in a partner-led or voucher motion. Facilitator note: resist letting this become an ECIF mechanics session - the mechanics only matter once the route is right.', N'Which investment route do you reach for by default?
When did an investment request last fail, and at what stage?
Who tells you whether something is eligible?', N'Before the session: bring one solution-area opportunity where customer investment might help.', N'What is the barrier the investment would actually remove?
Which route fits the size and shape of this deal?
What do you not know about eligibility?', N'Listen for one route being used for everything, for eligibility being assumed from a previous deal, for investment proposed before the barrier is clear, and for people who have stopped requesting because a past request failed late.', N'Move from the route to the eligibility question each person will route to an owner.
Customer Zero cue - use only if it happens: when someone reverts, rejects or overrides what the agent produced, stop and name it. Ask what the agent could not see. Huddle transcripts are reviewed for themes, so what is said here does reach us - keep customer-confidential detail out of it.', N'If nobody has a live candidate, take the last investment request that failed and work out which route it should have taken.', N'Close by confirming each person has a route and one eligibility question sent to a named owner.', N'What did you last build that turned out to be the wrong vehicle?', N'Before the next huddle, match one opportunity to an investment route and get one eligibility question answered authoritatively.', N'Bring the route you chose, the question you asked and the answer you got.'),
        (N'FG-PSS-INVEST', N'WF-INVEST-01', N'PSS-INVEST', N'The investment routes that matter most in this segment run through partners. This session is about using them deliberately.', N'Investment in this segment is a selection problem before it is a submission problem. There are several routes - direct customer investment, partner-led programs, and voucher-based offers - and most sellers learn one and use it for everything. The FY27 SME&C customer investment analysis records that Azure post-sales investment expects first-year ACR above roughly 500,000 US dollars, and that below that threshold the motion routes partner-led; it also records vouchers as the scale vehicle, at around 20,000 US dollars for Corporate against 50,000 for Enterprise. Treat those as orientation from that analysis rather than as current policy - confirm any figure with the authoritative owner before it reaches a customer. The point of the session is that choosing the wrong route wastes weeks that the right route would not have cost. For a PSS the additional dimension is that partner-led routes place obligations on the PARTNER - nomination, activity completion, evidence - and a partner who does not know what they have signed up to is the most common reason a partner-led investment produces nothing. Facilitator note: the partner''s incentive under each route is worth naming explicitly; it is rarely the same as ours.', N'Which partner-led routes do you actually use?
When did a partner-led program last produce nothing, and why?
Does the partner know what the program requires of them?', N'Before the session: bring one partner and one opportunity where investment could help.', N'What does this route require the partner to do?
What does the partner get out of it?
Who is tracking whether they do it?', N'Listen for partner obligations being assumed rather than agreed, for programs nominated and never started, for the partner''s incentive being taken for granted, and for PSS carrying activity that the program expects the partner to carry.', N'Move from the route to the specific obligation the partner has agreed to and the date.', N'If nobody has a live candidate, use a partner program that was nominated and never completed and work out where it stalled.', N'Close by confirming each person has a route and a partner-side obligation agreed with a name and a date.', N'Which partner program did you nominate and never follow up?', N'Before the next huddle, match one partner opportunity to a route and confirm with the partner what it requires of them.', N'Bring the route, what the partner agreed to, and whether they started.'),
        (N'FG-DAE-INVEST', N'WF-INVEST-01', N'DAE-INVEST', N'Customer investment reaches your role even though you do not own the request. This session is about the part that is yours.', N'Investment in this segment is a selection problem before it is a submission problem. There are several routes - direct customer investment, partner-led programs, and voucher-based offers - and most sellers learn one and use it for everything. The FY27 SME&C customer investment analysis records that Azure post-sales investment expects first-year ACR above roughly 500,000 US dollars, and that below that threshold the motion routes partner-led; it also records vouchers as the scale vehicle, at around 20,000 US dollars for Corporate against 50,000 for Enterprise. Treat those as orientation from that analysis rather than as current policy - confirm any figure with the authoritative owner before it reaches a customer. The point of the session is that choosing the wrong route wastes weeks that the right route would not have cost. A DAE does not own investment requests but meets the barriers that investment removes, across more accounts than anyone else. The value is in recognition and routing rather than in submission. Facilitator note: keep this session on the role''s own contribution rather than letting it drift into submission mechanics owned elsewhere.', N'Where have you seen investment help, and where have you seen it wasted?
What does your role actually contribute to an investment decision?
Who owns the request once you have raised it?', N'Before the session: bring one situation where customer investment is or was in play.', N'What is the barrier, precisely?
What is yours to decide here and what is not?
What would you need to see to be confident?', N'Listen for investment proposed as a discount by another name, for barriers that are really timing, and for DAEs building requests themselves rather than routing them.', N'Move from the assessment to the one thing each person routes or confirms this week.', N'If nobody has a live case, use the last investment situation they encountered and work through what their role should have contributed.', N'Close by naming what each person will route or confirm, and to whom.', N'When did you last see investment used for something it could not fix?', N'Before the next huddle, take one investment situation and contribute the part your role owns.', N'Bring what you found, who you took it to, and what happened.'),
        (N'FG-DSE-INVEST', N'WF-INVEST-01', N'DSE-INVEST', N'Customer investment reaches your role even though you do not own the request. This session is about the part that is yours.', N'Investment in this segment is a selection problem before it is a submission problem. There are several routes - direct customer investment, partner-led programs, and voucher-based offers - and most sellers learn one and use it for everything. The FY27 SME&C customer investment analysis records that Azure post-sales investment expects first-year ACR above roughly 500,000 US dollars, and that below that threshold the motion routes partner-led; it also records vouchers as the scale vehicle, at around 20,000 US dollars for Corporate against 50,000 for Enterprise. Treat those as orientation from that analysis rather than as current policy - confirm any figure with the authoritative owner before it reaches a customer. The point of the session is that choosing the wrong route wastes weeks that the right route would not have cost. A DSE does not own the investment request but owns whether the technical work it funds proves anything. Criteria set afterwards are always contested. Facilitator note: keep this session on the role''s own contribution rather than letting it drift into submission mechanics owned elsewhere.', N'Where have you seen investment help, and where have you seen it wasted?
What does your role actually contribute to an investment decision?
Who owns the request once you have raised it?', N'Before the session: bring one situation where customer investment is or was in play.', N'What is the barrier, precisely?
What is yours to decide here and what is not?
What would you need to see to be confident?', N'Listen for success criteria nobody could observe, for scope that expands to fill the funding, and for exit criteria being omitted entirely.', N'Move from the assessment to the one thing each person routes or confirms this week.', N'If nobody has a live case, use the last investment situation they encountered and work through what their role should have contributed.', N'Close by naming what each person will route or confirm, and to whom.', N'When did you last see investment used for something it could not fix?', N'Before the next huddle, take one investment situation and contribute the part your role owns.', N'Bring what you found, who you took it to, and what happened.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-INVEST', N'WF-INVEST-01', N'DCSA-INVEST', N'Customer investment reaches your role even though you do not own the request. This session is about the part that is yours.', N'Investment in this segment is a selection problem before it is a submission problem. There are several routes - direct customer investment, partner-led programs, and voucher-based offers - and most sellers learn one and use it for everything. The FY27 SME&C customer investment analysis records that Azure post-sales investment expects first-year ACR above roughly 500,000 US dollars, and that below that threshold the motion routes partner-led; it also records vouchers as the scale vehicle, at around 20,000 US dollars for Corporate against 50,000 for Enterprise. Treat those as orientation from that analysis rather than as current policy - confirm any figure with the authoritative owner before it reaches a customer. The point of the session is that choosing the wrong route wastes weeks that the right route would not have cost. A DCSA does not own the investment request but is accountable for whether the funded work converts into consumption. Non-unified customers have less capacity to absorb delivery than the request usually assumes. Facilitator note: keep this session on the role''s own contribution rather than letting it drift into submission mechanics owned elsewhere.', N'Where have you seen investment help, and where have you seen it wasted?
What does your role actually contribute to an investment decision?
Who owns the request once you have raised it?', N'Before the session: bring one situation where customer investment is or was in play.', N'What is the barrier, precisely?
What is yours to decide here and what is not?
What would you need to see to be confident?', N'Listen for prerequisites the customer has been told about but not agreed to, for partner delivery capacity being assumed, and for readiness judged from the opportunity rather than the environment.', N'Move from the assessment to the one thing each person routes or confirms this week.', N'If nobody has a live case, use the last investment situation they encountered and work through what their role should have contributed.', N'Close by naming what each person will route or confirm, and to whom.', N'When did you last see investment used for something it could not fix?', N'Before the next huddle, take one investment situation and contribute the part your role owns.', N'Bring what you found, who you took it to, and what happened.'),
        (N'FG-SMEC-CE-INVEST', N'WF-INVEST-01', N'SMEC-CE-INVEST', N'This is a short awareness session rather than a workflow. The CE does not own investment requests in this segment, but investment lands on deals you structure.', N'Investment in this segment is a selection problem before it is a submission problem. There are several routes - direct customer investment, partner-led programs, and voucher-based offers - and most sellers learn one and use it for everything. The FY27 SME&C customer investment analysis records that Azure post-sales investment expects first-year ACR above roughly 500,000 US dollars, and that below that threshold the motion routes partner-led; it also records vouchers as the scale vehicle, at around 20,000 US dollars for Corporate against 50,000 for Enterprise. Treat those as orientation from that analysis rather than as current policy - confirm any figure with the authoritative owner before it reaches a customer. The point of the session is that choosing the wrong route wastes weeks that the right route would not have cost. No source assigns the SME&C Commercial Executive ownership of investment requests, which is why this is an awareness card rather than a full workflow. What IS yours: whether an investment changes the commercial structure, whether it creates a precedent that the next similar customer will expect, and whether the structure still works if the investment is declined. Facilitator note: keep this short and do not let it become an ECIF mechanics session - route those questions to the roles that own them.', N'When has an investment changed a structure you built?
What would you want to know before structuring around one?
Who would you ask?', N'Before the session: think of one deal where investment was involved.', N'What would change about the structure if the investment did not come?
What precedent does this set?
Whose decision is the investment itself?', N'Listen for CEs taking on investment requests that belong elsewhere, for structures that only work if investment is approved, and for precedent implications going unnoticed.', N'Move from the discussion to the single question each person would raise next time.', N'If nobody has an example, use a deal with a non-standard term and ask what would have changed if investment had been part of it.', N'Close by naming the one question each person would raise if investment appeared on a deal they were structuring.', N'Have you ever built a structure that depended on funding you did not control?', N'Before the next huddle, if investment appears on a deal you are structuring, test whether the structure still holds without it.', N'Bring the deal, the dependency you found, and what you did about it.'),
        (N'FG-ALL-CLOSEDWON', N'WF-X-PIPE-01', N'ALL-CLOSEDWON', N'A closed-won record carries your number whether or not you were the one who set it. Closes arrive through your own work, through automation, and through other people acting on the account. This session is about reading your own recent closes and deciding which ones you can stand behind.', N'A wrong close raises no error and blocks nothing. It stays in the book until somebody has to explain it, and by then the context has gone.
The test is not whether the record is well-formed. It is whether the value, the products and the date describe what was actually sold.
Your account knowledge is the input here, not the verdict. A correction made without the contract in front of you replaces one wrong record with another.
Finding nothing is a real result. Say so in the room rather than treating the exercise as having failed.', N'When did you last read your own closed-won list rather than your open pipeline?
If a close in your book were wrong, how would you find out?
Which part of a closed record would you struggle to explain in a review?', N'Before the session: confirm you can open Sales Agent, and choose a recent period short enough to read in one sitting. Bring what you remember about the accounts in it, because that memory is the instrument this exercise runs on.', N'Which close in your list did you not recognise straight away?
What in that record does not match what you remember selling?
Is this one yours to correct, or does it need somebody else?', N'Listen for sellers scrolling past records they do not recognise because the totals look right. Listen for “that must have been the partner” or “that came from the team” offered as an explanation rather than as something to check. Listen for reluctance to raise a close that flatters the number, and for the assumption that a well-formed record is a correct one.', N'Move from the list of closes nobody could account for to the single record each person will resolve, and how they will resolve it.', N'If Sales Agent access is not available, run it from a report export or the opportunity list, since the exercise is reading your own closes rather than the tool that surfaces them. If nobody finds an unexplained close, use the time to ask how they would notice one.', N'Close by having each person name the one record they will resolve, and which of the four it is: accepted, corrected by them, routed to somebody who can act on it, or held pending a specific check.', N'What did you find in your own closed book that you had not looked at before? What would have to be true for you to notice it sooner next time?', N'Before the next huddle, resolve one closed-won record you could not account for: accept it, correct it, route it to whoever can act on it, or write down the specific thing you need to check.', N'Bring the record, what you could not account for, what you did about it, and whether it turned out to be yours to correct.'),
        (N'FG-CSA-VSAT', N'WF-X-VSAT-01', N'CSA-VSAT', N'Satisfaction is decided during the delivery, not at the survey. Today is about reading it early enough to act.', N'The customer already knows how they feel. The survey only tells us.
Responsiveness, clarity and expectation-setting move scores as much as technical outcomes do.
A satisfaction risk you cannot evidence is a feeling. Name what you saw.', N'Where in a current delivery would the customer say we are harder to work with than we need to be?
Which satisfaction signal have you noticed and not acted on?
What would this customer say if they were asked today rather than at the end?', N'Before the session: bring one live delivery, any customer feedback or escalation on it, and the moment you would least like the customer to remember.', N'Which delivery are you least confident about, and what is the evidence rather than the feeling?
What would the customer say we did slowly or explained badly?
Which satisfaction risk is yours to fix and which belongs to another role?', N'Through the CSA lens, listen for: satisfaction inferred from the absence of complaints; technical success treated as the whole of the customer experience; risks named without a specific moment behind them; and anything the CSA plans to absorb that belongs to the CSAM or to delivery.', N'Move from the general idea of satisfaction to one live delivery and the specific moment that would show up in the score.', N'Use a sanitized delivery. Do not speculate about individual customer contacts or attribute dissatisfaction to named people.', N'Name one satisfaction risk, the evidence behind it, and the action you will take before the delivery closes.', N'What did you learn today? Name one thing AI made easier, one thing that still needed your judgment, and one thing you would do differently next time.', N'Take one live delivery, name the satisfaction risk you would least like to see in the survey, and act on it this week.', N'Bring the risk you named and what changed after you acted.'),
        (N'FG-CSA-TECHLEAD', N'WF-X-TECHLEAD-01', N'CSA-TECHLEAD', N'You get called in when you are the person who can answer the question. Today is about deciding which questions those will be.', N'Technical credibility is set by the customer''s roadmap, not by our product releases.
The gap worth closing is the one the customer hits next, not the most interesting one.
Being able to say what you do not know is part of being trusted, not the opposite of it.', N'What will this customer be building in six months that you could not advise on today?
Where have you been the bottleneck because the answer had to come from someone else?
Which technical topic do you avoid, and what does avoiding it cost?', N'Before the session: bring one customer, what you know of their technical direction, and one question you were recently unable to answer.', N'What is the next technical decision this customer faces, and could you hold that conversation today?
Which gap is real and which is imagined?
What is the smallest step that would make you credible on it?', N'Through the CSA lens, listen for: gaps chosen by personal interest rather than by customer need; plans to learn everything rather than one thing; and cases where the honest answer is to bring in a specialist rather than close the gap personally.', N'Move from general capability to one customer, one upcoming technical decision, and one gap.', N'Use a sanitized customer. Keep the focus on technical direction rather than commercial detail.', N'Name the technical gap you are closing, why this customer makes it urgent, and the first step.', N'What did you learn today? Name one thing AI made easier, one thing that still needed your judgment, and one thing you would do differently next time.', N'Name one technical topic your customer will need you on within the next two quarters, and take the first step toward being able to defend it.', N'Bring the gap you named and what you did about it.')
    ) v([ExternalId], [TopicExternalId], [PlacementExternalId], [SessionIntroduction], [KeyTalkingPoints], [DiscussionQuestions], [PreparationChecklist], [FacilitatorQuestions], [ListenFor], [SuggestedTransitions], [FallbackGuidance], [WrapUpGuidance], [ReflectPrompt], [CommitPrompt], [BringBackEvidence])
), src AS (
    SELECT raw.*, t.Id AS [HuddleTopicId], p.Id AS [HuddlePlacementId]
    FROM raw
    INNER JOIN dbo.HuddleTopics AS t ON t.[ExternalId] = raw.[TopicExternalId]
    INNER JOIN dbo.HuddlePlacements AS p ON p.[ExternalId] = raw.[PlacementExternalId]
)
MERGE dbo.HuddleFacilitatorGuides AS tgt
USING src ON tgt.[ExternalId] = src.[ExternalId]
WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET
        tgt.[HuddleTopicId] = src.[HuddleTopicId],
        tgt.[HuddlePlacementId] = src.[HuddlePlacementId],
        tgt.[SessionIntroduction] = src.[SessionIntroduction],
        tgt.[KeyTalkingPoints] = src.[KeyTalkingPoints],
        tgt.[DiscussionQuestions] = src.[DiscussionQuestions],
        tgt.[PreparationChecklist] = src.[PreparationChecklist],
        tgt.[FacilitatorQuestions] = src.[FacilitatorQuestions],
        tgt.[ListenFor] = src.[ListenFor],
        tgt.[SuggestedTransitions] = src.[SuggestedTransitions],
        tgt.[FallbackGuidance] = src.[FallbackGuidance],
        tgt.[WrapUpGuidance] = src.[WrapUpGuidance],
        tgt.[ReflectPrompt] = src.[ReflectPrompt],
        tgt.[CommitPrompt] = src.[CommitPrompt],
        tgt.[BringBackEvidence] = src.[BringBackEvidence],
        tgt.[UpdatedAtUtc] = SYSUTCDATETIME()
WHEN NOT MATCHED BY TARGET THEN INSERT ([ExternalId], [HuddleTopicId], [HuddlePlacementId], [SessionIntroduction], [KeyTalkingPoints], [DiscussionQuestions], [PreparationChecklist], [FacilitatorQuestions], [ListenFor], [SuggestedTransitions], [FallbackGuidance], [WrapUpGuidance], [ReflectPrompt], [CommitPrompt], [BringBackEvidence], [CreatedAtUtc])
    VALUES (src.[ExternalId], src.[HuddleTopicId], src.[HuddlePlacementId], src.[SessionIntroduction], src.[KeyTalkingPoints], src.[DiscussionQuestions], src.[PreparationChecklist], src.[FacilitatorQuestions], src.[ListenFor], src.[SuggestedTransitions], src.[FallbackGuidance], src.[WrapUpGuidance], src.[ReflectPrompt], src.[CommitPrompt], src.[BringBackEvidence], SYSUTCDATETIME());
;WITH raw AS (
    SELECT * FROM (VALUES
        (N'FG-DSE-ORIENT', N'WF-ORIENT-01', N'DSE-ORIENT', N'This is the first of eight huddles, and the one with no AI activity in it. Today is about what the motion is and what it asks of you. Frontier Accelerator exists because we are asking customers to reshape how they work around AI, and that argument is stronger when we have done it ourselves first. That is what Customer Zero means here: we apply these tools to our own real work, and we are straight about where they help and where they do not.', N'Weekly and short, led by the manager with a facilitator. The cadence carries the motion, so if the slot has to move, move it rather than drop it.
Four principles hold every week: keep the weekly rhythm, learn from what colleagues have actually tried, ground every session in real work rather than hypothetical scenarios, and try something between huddles so there is an experience to bring back.
Every huddle from next week runs to the same shape, and the agenda and timings are set out on the huddle page: a short framing of why the topic matters, then sharing what you tried since last time, then hands-on work on a real scenario, then a close where you pick the one thing you will carry into the week.
Five roles make the motion work. The Leader connects it to strategy and clears barriers. The Manager owns the huddle and opens with the why. The Facilitator runs it and draws every voice in. The Team Member experiments in their own workflow and brings back wins and friction. The Coach reviews patterns across huddles and feeds insight back.
The journey ahead: this week you get grounded, weeks two to eight you build the habit on real work, week nine the team decides which habits stick and what comes next, and from week ten you choose your own topics.
You are here as a peer, not an expert. You do not need prior AI experience, and “I do not know” is a good answer.
Two things on feedback. The facilitator posts one question in the chat each week, asking how useful the huddle was on a scale of one to ten. Huddle transcripts are reviewed for themes to improve the sessions, so keep confidential detail out of the room, whether that is customer, partner, enrollment or commercial, and put product problems into the tool''s own feedback control rather than here.', N'Which part of your week is assembling information rather than deciding anything?
Where do you already use AI, and where have you deliberately avoided it?
What would you need to see before you trusted an AI-drafted output in front of a customer?
What would make thirty minutes a week worth protecting for this?', N'Before the session: complete the baseline survey if it has been sent to you, confirm the huddle series is on your calendar, confirm you can open at least one of the tools named for your path, since the commitment at the end of this session runs in one of them, and come ready to describe the work you repeat most weeks. If your access is blocked, come anyway and say so, because that is a different problem from not being interested. No prior AI experience is needed for this session.', N'Which work do you repeat every week without ever thinking of it as a workflow?
Which of those steps is assembling information rather than making a decision?
Where does your judgment have to stay human no matter how good the output gets?
What would have to happen in these thirty minutes for you to want the next one?', N'Listen for unvoiced skepticism, for access blockers stated as disinterest, and for the assumption that this is a tool rollout. Listen for people describing only their exceptional work rather than the work they repeat, because the recurring and unglamorous tasks are the ones worth mapping. Listen for anyone who thinks they need to be an AI expert to take part, and correct that in the room.', N'Move from how the motion works to the recurring work each person will map with the discovery prompt this week.', N'If tool access is not confirmed, still run the conversation and record access blockers separately from interest, since they need different fixes. The discovery prompt runs in whichever tool the person already has. If the room stays quiet, start with your own example of work you repeat every week.', N'Close by pointing the team to the resources for next week''s topic and to the discovery prompt below. Week one is for finding the recurring work worth handing to AI. Post the usefulness question in the chat before people leave.', N'Which part of your week had you never thought of as a workflow until now? What would have to be true before you trusted AI with it?', N'Run this in Microsoft 365 Copilot, Cowork, or Scout before the next huddle, then bring the result to the session:

“I am a [your role] at Microsoft and I want to find the recurring work in my week that AI could take on.
1. First, look at my calendar, mail and Teams activity from the last four weeks and tell me what you can see about where my time actually goes. If you cannot access that data, say so and move to step 2 rather than guessing.
2. Then ask me up to five short questions, one at a time, to cover the work that never shows up in a calendar or an inbox.
3. Then list the five things I do most often. For each one give an estimate of the time it costs me per month, whether it is best handled as a saved prompt, a reusable skill, a scheduled automation or an existing agent, and the one judgment that must stay mine.
4. Rank them by time returned and tell me which one to start with.”', N'Bring the ranked list of the five things you repeat most, which of them looked like a prompt, skill, automation or agent fit, and any AI experience from the week, including anything that did not work.'),
        (N'FG-DSE-DISC', N'WF-X-DISC-01', N'DSE-DISC', N'Somebody has usually asked this customer most of your questions already. This session is about finding out what they said before you ask again.', N'In this segment a partner is frequently engaged before the engineer is, and may have run discovery of their own. The DAE owns the account relationship and the DSS covers the solution area, so three people may hold pieces of the same picture. Partner-side conversations are not in MSX and return through the Partner Timeline view or Partner Center. Facilitator note: the failure mode here is not missing information, it is asking for information somebody already has, which reads to the customer as a team that does not talk to itself.', N'How do you currently find out what has already been asked?
When did you last repeat a question the customer had already answered?
What do you need to establish yourself, whatever anyone else has done?', N'Before the session: bring one customer where you are about to start technical discovery.', N'What does the record already tell you about this customer''s estate?
Who else has spoken to them, and what would they know that is not written down?
What must you see for yourself rather than take on trust?', N'Listen for discovery restarted from scratch out of habit, for partner-held knowledge assumed to be unavailable rather than asked for, for the record treated as complete, and for engineers accepting a second-hand answer on something they will be accountable for.', N'Move from what is already known to the short list only this person can establish.', N'If nobody has upcoming discovery, use the last engagement where something important surfaced late and work out who already knew it.', N'Close by naming the one thing each person will ask a colleague or partner rather than the customer.', N'What were you about to ask a customer that somebody on your own side already knew?', N'Before the next huddle, establish what is already known about one customer''s estate from the record and from whoever else is engaged, and take only your genuinely unanswered questions to the customer.', N'Bring what you learned without asking the customer, and what you still had to ask.'),
        (N'FG-DSE-PROOF', N'WF-X-ARCH-01', N'DSE-PROOF', N'Building a bespoke proof for every customer does not survive contact with this many customers. This session is about proving the point with less.', N'Enterprise engineers can afford a tailored proof per pursuit; at this customer count that arithmetic does not work, so reuse is the constraint rather than a nice-to-have. A partner may be able to run the proof instead, which is covered separately in the design session. What matters here is deciding the smallest thing that would actually settle the customer''s doubt. Facilitator note: the most common failure is building a technically impressive proof of something the customer was never doubting.', N'How long does a proof take you, and how many can you run at once?
When did you last build something that already existed?
What was the customer actually unsure about?', N'Before the session: bring one customer asking for a demo, pilot or proof.', N'What decision is this proof supposed to settle?
Does something already exist that would settle it?
What is the smallest version that would still be convincing?', N'Listen for proofs scoped to what is interesting to build rather than to what is in doubt, for reuse dismissed without looking, for success criteria that were never agreed with the customer, and for effort that exceeds the value of the deal.', N'Move from the decision in doubt to what each person will reuse or scope.', N'If nobody has a live request, use the last proof that was built and work out what the smallest sufficient version would have been.', N'Close by naming what each person is reusing rather than building.', N'What did you build that you could have borrowed?', N'Before the next huddle, name the decision one customer''s proof must settle, look for something that already exists before building anything, and scope the smallest sufficient version.', N'Bring what you found already existing, and what you still had to build.'),
        (N'FG-DSE-CONV', N'WF-X-CONV-01', N'DSE-CONV', N'Most technical questions reach you second-hand, and your answer goes back the same way. This session is about writing for the relay.', N'At this scale the engineer is frequently not in the room. A partner or a seller carries the question in and the answer back, which means the question has already been through one translation before you see it and the answer goes through another on the way out. CELA rules govern what may be exchanged with a partner: personal data, MSX screenshots and exports, and financial or seat-level detail are off-limits. Facilitator note: an answer that requires your presence to make sense is not finished, because you will not be there.', N'How often are you answering a question the customer asked somebody else?
When has an answer of yours arrived at a customer distorted?
What do you put in writing differently when a partner will forward it?', N'Before the session: bring one technical question that reached you through somebody else.', N'What do you think the customer actually asked, as opposed to what reached you?
Which part of your answer would you least like to see paraphrased?
What would this look like if the person relaying it did not understand it?', N'Listen for the relayed question taken at face value, for answers written for a technical peer rather than for the relayer, for caveats that will not survive being summarized, and for detail that CELA rules exclude from partner exchange.', N'Move from the reconstructed question to the answer each person will write for relay.', N'If nobody has a live question, use one that came back a second time and work out what was lost in the first answer.', N'Close by naming the part of each answer that must not be paraphrased.', N'Which of your answers has been repeated to a customer in a form you would not recognize?', N'Before the next huddle, answer one relayed technical question in a form that survives being forwarded, and mark explicitly what must be passed on word for word.', N'Bring the answer you wrote and what came back.'),
        (N'FG-DSE-HEALTH', N'WF-X-HEALTH-01', N'DSE-HEALTH', N'You will not notice a customer''s technical problem by looking at them, because you are not looking at most of them and you do not operate any of it. You notice by recognizing a pattern you have already met.', N'The technical estate here is operated by partners and customers rather than by us, and there are far more customers per engineer than in Enterprise, so risk is found by pattern rather than by inspection. The problems worth tracking are the ones you have already diagnosed once: a configuration that fails under load, a version combination that breaks on upgrade, an integration that degrades quietly. Consumption movement belongs to the D-CSA and is not the engineer''s signal. The intervention normally runs through whoever operates the solution. Facilitator note: the judgment here is about noticing and routing rather than fixing.', N'How would you currently find out a customer''s solution was in trouble?
Which technical problem have you now seen at more than one customer?
Who actually fixes it when you find one?', N'Before the session: bring one technical problem you have seen at more than one customer.', N'Which of your customers are running the configuration you already know is fragile?
Which of them has a partner who probably knows already?
What can you actually do about it from where you sit?', N'Listen for problems treated as one-offs when they have already recurred, for partner-operated estates assumed to be unmonitored, for engineers taking on a fix that belongs to whoever operates it, and for warnings sent without a route to action.', N'Move from the exposed list to the one fix each person routes.', N'If nobody has a recurring problem, use the last customer escalation and ask whether anything about it was predictable.', N'Close by naming the customer each person is warning and who is actually going to fix it.', N'Which problem had you already seen twice without treating it as a pattern?', N'Before the next huddle, identify which of your customers are exposed to a technical problem you have already met elsewhere, and route one fix to whoever operates the solution.', N'Bring the pattern, who was exposed, and where you routed it.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-ORIENT', N'WF-ORIENT-01', N'DCSA-ORIENT', N'This is the first of eight huddles, and the one with no AI activity in it. Today is about what the motion is and what it asks of you. Frontier Accelerator exists because we are asking customers to reshape how they work around AI, and that argument is stronger when we have done it ourselves first. That is what Customer Zero means here: we apply these tools to our own real work, and we are straight about where they help and where they do not.', N'Weekly and short, led by the manager with a facilitator. The cadence carries the motion, so if the slot has to move, move it rather than drop it.
Four principles hold every week: keep the weekly rhythm, learn from what colleagues have actually tried, ground every session in real work rather than hypothetical scenarios, and try something between huddles so there is an experience to bring back.
Every huddle from next week runs to the same shape, and the agenda and timings are set out on the huddle page: a short framing of why the topic matters, then sharing what you tried since last time, then hands-on work on a real scenario, then a close where you pick the one thing you will carry into the week.
Five roles make the motion work. The Leader connects it to strategy and clears barriers. The Manager owns the huddle and opens with the why. The Facilitator runs it and draws every voice in. The Team Member experiments in their own workflow and brings back wins and friction. The Coach reviews patterns across huddles and feeds insight back.
The journey ahead: this week you get grounded, weeks two to eight you build the habit on real work, week nine the team decides which habits stick and what comes next, and from week ten you choose your own topics.
You are here as a peer, not an expert. You do not need prior AI experience, and “I do not know” is a good answer.
Two things on feedback. The facilitator posts one question in the chat each week, asking how useful the huddle was on a scale of one to ten. Huddle transcripts are reviewed for themes to improve the sessions, so keep confidential detail out of the room, whether that is customer, partner, enrollment or commercial, and put product problems into the tool''s own feedback control rather than here.', N'Which part of your week is assembling information rather than deciding anything?
Where do you already use AI, and where have you deliberately avoided it?
What would you need to see before you trusted an AI-drafted output in front of a customer?
What would make thirty minutes a week worth protecting for this?', N'Before the session: complete the baseline survey if it has been sent to you, confirm the huddle series is on your calendar, confirm you can open at least one of the tools named for your path, since the commitment at the end of this session runs in one of them, and come ready to describe the work you repeat most weeks. If your access is blocked, come anyway and say so, because that is a different problem from not being interested. No prior AI experience is needed for this session.', N'Which work do you repeat every week without ever thinking of it as a workflow?
Which of those steps is assembling information rather than making a decision?
Where does your judgment have to stay human no matter how good the output gets?
What would have to happen in these thirty minutes for you to want the next one?', N'Listen for unvoiced skepticism, for access blockers stated as disinterest, and for the assumption that this is a tool rollout. Listen for people describing only their exceptional work rather than the work they repeat, because the recurring and unglamorous tasks are the ones worth mapping. Listen for anyone who thinks they need to be an AI expert to take part, and correct that in the room.', N'Move from how the motion works to the recurring work each person will map with the discovery prompt this week.', N'If tool access is not confirmed, still run the conversation and record access blockers separately from interest, since they need different fixes. The discovery prompt runs in whichever tool the person already has. If the room stays quiet, start with your own example of work you repeat every week.', N'Close by pointing the team to the resources for next week''s topic and to the discovery prompt below. Week one is for finding the recurring work worth handing to AI. Post the usefulness question in the chat before people leave.', N'Which part of your week had you never thought of as a workflow until now? What would have to be true before you trusted AI with it?', N'Run this in Microsoft 365 Copilot, Cowork, or Scout before the next huddle, then bring the result to the session:

“I am a [your role] at Microsoft and I want to find the recurring work in my week that AI could take on.
1. First, look at my calendar, mail and Teams activity from the last four weeks and tell me what you can see about where my time actually goes. If you cannot access that data, say so and move to step 2 rather than guessing.
2. Then ask me up to five short questions, one at a time, to cover the work that never shows up in a calendar or an inbox.
3. Then list the five things I do most often. For each one give an estimate of the time it costs me per month, whether it is best handled as a saved prompt, a reusable skill, a scheduled automation or an existing agent, and the one judgment that must stay mine.
4. Rank them by time returned and tell me which one to start with.”', N'Bring the ranked list of the five things you repeat most, which of them looked like a prompt, skill, automation or agent fit, and any AI experience from the week, including anything that did not work.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-PARTNER', N'WF-X-PARTNER-01', N'DCSA-PARTNER', N'On these accounts the technical work is done by a partner you have no authority over, with no support agreement behind you. This session is about making a handover that actually gets acted on.', N'Consumption execution in this segment runs through the D-CSA and partner delivery, particularly on non-Unified Azure. Unlike a Unified engagement there is no funded delivery vehicle and no contractual escalation route, so the handover has to stand on its own. Partner Center sits outside what our agents can reach, so partner-side progress returns through the Partner Timeline view or Partner Center directly. CELA rules govern the exchange: personal data, MSX screenshots and exports, and financial or seat-level detail are off-limits. Facilitator note: the useful frame is that you are writing for a competent engineer who was not in any of your conversations.', N'When you hand technical work to a partner, how do you find out whether it happened?
What do you end up doing yourself because handing it over felt slower?
What does a partner need from you that you usually leave out?', N'Before the session: bring one account where a partner is doing or should be doing the technical work.', N'What would this partner need to act on this without calling you?
How would you know in a month whether anything happened?
What are you holding onto that you should have handed over?', N'Listen for handovers that assume context the partner does not have, for work retained because delegating felt slower, for progress assumed from silence, and for detail being prepared that CELA rules exclude.', N'Move from the handover to the signal each person will use to check it landed.', N'If nobody has a live handover, use the last one that stalled and work out what was missing from it.', N'Close by naming what each person is handing over and the signal they will check.', N'What did you keep because explaining it felt like more work than doing it?', N'Before the next huddle, hand one piece of technical work to a partner in a form they can act on unaided, and write down the specific signal you will check to know whether it worked.', N'Bring what you handed over and what the signal showed.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-CONV', N'WF-X-CONV-01', N'DCSA-CONV', N'You are probably not coming back to this account. This session is about preparing for a conversation on that assumption.', N'A Unified engagement supports an ongoing advisory relationship with recurring contact. At this coverage ratio and without that agreement, most customers get a single technical conversation and then nothing until something breaks. That changes the objective from building understanding over time to making one intervention that survives your absence. Facilitator note: the temptation is to cover everything because you may not be back, and that reliably produces a conversation the customer remembers nothing from.', N'How many of your accounts have you spoken to more than once?
What happens at an account after you leave it?
When did you last try to cover everything in one meeting?', N'Before the session: bring one customer you have a technical conversation coming up with.', N'What is the single thing this customer most needs to get right?
Who will be here after you are not?
What would still be true in six months if you did nothing else?', N'Listen for agendas that try to cover everything, for advice that depends on the architect returning, for no named owner on the customer or partner side, and for durable guidance never written down.', N'Move from the single priority to what each person will leave behind.', N'If nobody has an upcoming conversation, use an account they have not revisited and ask what is still standing from the last one.', N'Close by naming the one thing each person is leaving behind and who is picking it up.', N'Which of your advice quietly assumed you would be coming back?', N'Before the next huddle, prepare one customer conversation around a single priority, and leave something written that a customer or partner could act on without you.', N'Bring what you left behind and who owns it now.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-VALUE', N'WF-X-VALUE-01', N'DCSA-VALUE', N'Somebody else delivered most of what worked at your accounts. This session is about what you can honestly say about it.', N'A Unified engagement leaves a delivery record - milestones, hours, an engagement manager - that can be pointed at. On non-Unified accounts none of that exists, so the evidence is telemetry plus whatever the partner is willing to share. Separate what was deployed from what is being used and from what the customer got value from; those are three different claims and only the first is reliably in the record. The renewal conversation belongs to the DAE and the CE, so evidence that stays with you does not reach it. Facilitator note: partner-delivered outcomes are the partner''s to describe, and claiming them damages the relationship that is doing the work.', N'When an account goes well, where is the proof?
How much of what worked was delivered by a partner?
Who asks you for value evidence, and when?', N'Before the session: bring two or three accounts where you believe the technical outcome was good.', N'What can you evidence about that outcome as opposed to believe?
Whose outcome is it to describe?
Who needs this before the renewal, and have they got it?', N'Listen for deployment presented as value, for partner-delivered outcomes claimed as ours, for evidence that exists only in the architect''s head, and for renewals approaching with nobody having asked.', N'Move from the evidence review to what each person passes on and to whom.', N'If nobody has a clear success, use an account that renewed quietly and ask what evidence would have been available if anyone had wanted it.', N'Close by naming what each person is passing on and who is receiving it.', N'Which good outcome would have gone unrecorded if nobody asked?', N'Before the next huddle, establish what one account''s technical outcome can actually be evidenced with, separate what the partner delivered, and send it to whoever owns the renewal.', N'Bring what you could evidence, what you could not, and who you sent it to.'),
        (N'FG-PSS-ORIENT', N'WF-ORIENT-01', N'PSS-ORIENT', N'This is the first of eight huddles, and the one with no AI activity in it. Today is about what the motion is and what it asks of you. Frontier Accelerator exists because we are asking customers to reshape how they work around AI, and that argument is stronger when we have done it ourselves first. That is what Customer Zero means here: we apply these tools to our own real work, and we are straight about where they help and where they do not.', N'Weekly and short, led by the manager with a facilitator. The cadence carries the motion, so if the slot has to move, move it rather than drop it.
Four principles hold every week: keep the weekly rhythm, learn from what colleagues have actually tried, ground every session in real work rather than hypothetical scenarios, and try something between huddles so there is an experience to bring back.
Every huddle from next week runs to the same shape, and the agenda and timings are set out on the huddle page: a short framing of why the topic matters, then sharing what you tried since last time, then hands-on work on a real scenario, then a close where you pick the one thing you will carry into the week.
Five roles make the motion work. The Leader connects it to strategy and clears barriers. The Manager owns the huddle and opens with the why. The Facilitator runs it and draws every voice in. The Team Member experiments in their own workflow and brings back wins and friction. The Coach reviews patterns across huddles and feeds insight back.
The journey ahead: this week you get grounded, weeks two to eight you build the habit on real work, week nine the team decides which habits stick and what comes next, and from week ten you choose your own topics.
You are here as a peer, not an expert. You do not need prior AI experience, and “I do not know” is a good answer.
Two things on feedback. The facilitator posts one question in the chat each week, asking how useful the huddle was on a scale of one to ten. Huddle transcripts are reviewed for themes to improve the sessions, so keep confidential detail out of the room, whether that is customer, partner, enrollment or commercial, and put product problems into the tool''s own feedback control rather than here.', N'Which part of your week is assembling information rather than deciding anything?
Where do you already use AI, and where have you deliberately avoided it?
What would you need to see before you trusted an AI-drafted output in front of a customer?
What would make thirty minutes a week worth protecting for this?', N'Before the session: complete the baseline survey if it has been sent to you, confirm the huddle series is on your calendar, confirm you can open at least one of the tools named for your path, since the commitment at the end of this session runs in one of them, and come ready to describe the work you repeat most weeks. If your access is blocked, come anyway and say so, because that is a different problem from not being interested. No prior AI experience is needed for this session.', N'Which work do you repeat every week without ever thinking of it as a workflow?
Which of those steps is assembling information rather than making a decision?
Where does your judgment have to stay human no matter how good the output gets?
What would have to happen in these thirty minutes for you to want the next one?', N'Listen for unvoiced skepticism, for access blockers stated as disinterest, and for the assumption that this is a tool rollout. Listen for people describing only their exceptional work rather than the work they repeat, because the recurring and unglamorous tasks are the ones worth mapping. Listen for anyone who thinks they need to be an AI expert to take part, and correct that in the room.', N'Move from how the motion works to the recurring work each person will map with the discovery prompt this week.', N'If tool access is not confirmed, still run the conversation and record access blockers separately from interest, since they need different fixes. The discovery prompt runs in whichever tool the person already has. If the room stays quiet, start with your own example of work you repeat every week.', N'Close by pointing the team to the resources for next week''s topic and to the discovery prompt below. Week one is for finding the recurring work worth handing to AI. Post the usefulness question in the chat before people leave.', N'Which part of your week had you never thought of as a workflow until now? What would have to be true before you trusted AI with it?', N'Run this in Microsoft 365 Copilot, Cowork, or Scout before the next huddle, then bring the result to the session:

“I am a [your role] at Microsoft and I want to find the recurring work in my week that AI could take on.
1. First, look at my calendar, mail and Teams activity from the last four weeks and tell me what you can see about where my time actually goes. If you cannot access that data, say so and move to step 2 rather than guessing.
2. Then ask me up to five short questions, one at a time, to cover the work that never shows up in a calendar or an inbox.
3. Then list the five things I do most often. For each one give an estimate of the time it costs me per month, whether it is best handled as a saved prompt, a reusable skill, a scheduled automation or an existing agent, and the one judgment that must stay mine.
4. Rank them by time returned and tell me which one to start with.”', N'Bring the ranked list of the five things you repeat most, which of them looked like a prompt, skill, automation or agent fit, and any AI experience from the week, including anything that did not work.'),
        (N'FG-PSS-DEMAND', N'WF-X-OPP-01', N'PSS-DEMAND', N'Most roles here decide what to send out to a partner. You also have to decide what to do with what comes back the other way.', N'Partner-originated demand arrives qualified to the partner''s standard rather than ours, and the windows are already running: a partner has fourteen days to accept a referral before it expires, and sixty days to convert it before the system marks it lost. IP co-sell registration has its own conditions - the deal must be won on a Microsoft-managed account, be Azure IP co-sell eligible, be at least twenty-five thousand US dollars, and have a seventy-two hour gap between creation and being marked won. CELA rules limit what can be exchanged in either direction. Facilitator note: the honest difficulty here is that you often cannot verify what the partner is telling you, and deciding how much to invest on their word is the actual skill.', N'How much of your pipeline originates with a partner rather than with us?
How do you decide whether to believe a partner''s qualification?
When did a partner-sourced deal turn out not to be real?', N'Before the session: bring one opportunity that came to you from a partner.', N'What do you actually know about this opportunity as opposed to what you were told?
What would you need to see to invest Microsoft resource behind it?
Which of your open partner-sourced items are closest to a window closing?', N'Listen for partner qualification accepted at face value, for registration conditions assumed rather than checked, for windows running unnoticed, and for reluctance to push back on a partner in case it damages the relationship.', N'Move from the verification read to the one opportunity each person progresses or declines.', N'If nobody has a live partner-sourced opportunity, use the last one that went nowhere and work out what would have revealed it earlier.', N'Close by naming the opportunity each person is progressing and the one they are declining.', N'Which partner-sourced opportunity have you been carrying on trust?', N'Before the next huddle, establish what can actually be verified about one partner-originated opportunity, check its registration conditions, and either progress it or tell the partner why you are not.', N'Bring what you could verify, what you could not, and what you decided.'),
        (N'FG-PSS-ORCH', N'WF-X-ORCH-01', N'PSS-ORCH', N'A cadence commitment across a portfolio of partners becomes a diary full of status calls unless somebody decides what each one is for. This session is about that decision.', N'The partner rhythm in this segment includes a weekly partner meeting with the account roles and a minimum of monthly contact per partner seller, alongside deal clinics and the bi-weekly territory health review. Meeting a minimum is not the same as running a portfolio, and the partners who most need attention are rarely the ones who ask for it. Facilitator note: the useful distinction is between a meeting that needs an agenda because something has to be decided, and one that needs only a check-in because nothing has changed - treating them the same is what fills the week.', N'How many partner meetings do you hold in a month, and how many changed anything?
Which partner have you not spoken to in longer than you should admit?
What makes a partner meeting worth having?', N'Before the session: bring your partner list and roughly when you last spoke to each.', N'Which of your partners has something that actually needs deciding this month?
Which are you meeting out of obligation?
Who is quietly getting no attention at all?', N'Listen for cadence treated as the objective rather than the floor, for the loudest partners absorbing the time, for meetings with no decision attached, and for the quiet partners going unmentioned.', N'Move from the triage to the agenda each person will set for their one substantive meeting.', N'If someone has few partners, work the last month of meetings and ask which produced a decision.', N'Close by naming the partner each person is giving real time to and the ones they are handling lightly.', N'Which partner has been getting your time because they ask for it rather than because they need it?', N'Before the next huddle, triage three to five of your partners for the month, set a real agenda for the one meeting that needs a decision, and make sure your monthly contact commitment is met across the partner sellers you carry.', N'Bring your triage and what the substantive meeting decided.'),
        (N'FG-PSS-VALUE', N'WF-X-VALUE-01', N'PSS-VALUE', N'When a partner closes and delivers, Microsoft largely stops seeing the customer. You are the only route back.', N'In CSP the partner frequently leads the later stages and often closes, which means our record ends roughly where the customer''s experience begins. The PSS has visibility into the partner relationship without owning execution, so the realistic goal is closing an information gap rather than driving an outcome. What the partner will tell you is governed by the CELA rules in both directions, and a partner is under no obligation to report customer outcomes to us at all. Facilitator note: the value of asking is as much about the partner relationship as about the answer - a partner who is asked how the customer is doing behaves differently from one who is only asked about pipeline.', N'What do you know about customers your partners closed last quarter?
Who would tell you if one of them was unhappy?
When did you last ask a partner about a customer rather than about a deal?', N'Before the session: bring two or three deals a partner closed in the last few quarters.', N'What does our record actually show about these customers since the close?
What would you want to know that only the partner can tell you?
What is in it for the partner to tell you?', N'Listen for usage data mistaken for customer satisfaction, for the assumption that silence means success, for questions framed as inspection rather than as interest, and for renewal dates approaching with nobody having asked anything.', N'Move from the visibility gap to the one question each person will put to a partner.', N'If nobody has recent closed deals, use a renewal that was lost and ask when the first sign was available.', N'Close by naming the partner each person is asking and the question they are asking.', N'Which customer do you know nothing about since the day it closed?', N'Before the next huddle, map what our record can and cannot tell you about customers a partner closed, and ask one partner directly how a specific customer is getting on.', N'Bring what you could not see, what the partner told you, and whether it surprised you.'),
        (N'FG-SMEC-CE-POLICY', N'WF-X-POLICY-01', N'SMEC-CE-POLICY', N'Every deal meets the policy questions eventually. This session is about meeting them while the structure is still a question rather than a commitment.', N'Policy, licensing and eligibility interpretation always routes to the authoritative owner rather than to an agent or to your own reading. In this segment a substantial share transacts through CSP, which carries its own eligibility conditions - Modern Commerce and an accepted agreement with a pre-existing partner billing relationship, with legacy arrangements excluded - so the route matters as much as the structure. Facilitator note: the failure mode is not getting a policy answer wrong, it is answering at all when the question belonged to somebody else, because an answer from the commercial person is treated as authoritative.', N'When did a policy question last surface too late to change anything?
Which questions do you find yourself answering that you should be routing?
How long does an authoritative answer actually take to get?', N'Before the session: bring one live deal where the commercial structure is not yet fixed.', N'What in this deal has an eligibility or licensing condition attached to it?
Which of those can you answer from approved guidance, and which need the owner?
What would it cost to find out at approval instead of now?', N'Listen for policy answered from memory or from a previous deal, for eligibility assumed because a similar customer qualified, for CSP conditions treated as the same as direct, and for questions deferred because getting an answer feels slow.', N'Move from the question list to the one each person will route today.', N'If nobody has a live deal, use the last one where a policy issue surfaced late and work out when it first became answerable.', N'Close by naming the question each person is routing and who they are routing it to.', N'Which policy question have you been answering yourself?', N'Before the next huddle, run one live deal against the policy and eligibility questions it will face, and route every unresolved one to its authoritative owner rather than carrying it.', N'Bring the question you routed and what the answer changed.'),
        (N'FG-SMEC-CE-NEG', N'WF-X-DEAL-01', N'SMEC-CE-NEG', N'In this segment the negotiation is frequently conducted by a partner. Your concession logic has to work when applied by somebody who does not report to you.', N'The Commercial Executive owns concession authority and the commercial boundary, but in a partner-transacted deal the conversation with the customer is often the partner''s. That changes the artefact from a negotiation plan you will execute into a boundary somebody else has to hold. CELA rules govern what can be shared with the partner: personal data, screenshots and exports, and financial or seat-level detail are off-limits. Facilitator note: the useful question in the room is what happens to your boundary when you are not there to defend it.', N'On your last partner-transacted deal, who actually did the negotiating?
What did you concede that you had not planned to?
How do you tell a partner what they cannot offer without telling them your floor?', N'Before the session: bring one live deal heading toward a negotiation.', N'What can be conceded on this deal without approval, and what cannot?
How does the partner know where that line is?
What would trigger you to escalate rather than concede?', N'Listen for concession authority assumed rather than confirmed, for boundaries that exist only in the CE''s head, for partner briefings that reveal the floor, and for escalation triggers defined after the pressure has started.', N'Move from the boundary to how each person will communicate it to the partner.', N'If nobody has a live negotiation, use one that conceded more than intended and work out where the boundary failed.', N'Close by naming the boundary each person is setting and how the partner will learn it.', N'Where has a concession been made in your name that you would not have made?', N'Before the next huddle, write down the concession logic and authority limits for one live deal, name the escalation trigger, and brief the partner on what cannot be conceded without revealing your floor.', N'Bring what you told the partner and whether the boundary held.'),
        (N'FG-SMEC-CE-ORCH', N'WF-X-ORCH-01', N'SMEC-CE-ORCH', N'Approvals discovered one at a time cost a week each. This session is about finding them all at once, including the ones that are not yours to obtain.', N'On a partner-transacted deal the approval path runs across two organizations, and the steps on the partner side are visible only through the Partner Timeline view or Partner Center rather than through our own systems. Approval lead times drift with quarter load, so a plan built on last quarter''s turnaround will miss. Facilitator note: the distinction worth drawing is between an approval you can obtain, one you can only request, and one the partner has to obtain from their own side - all three look the same on a plan and behave completely differently.', N'How do you currently find out what approvals a structure will need?
Which approvals on your last deal were discovered late?
What happens when the partner needs an approval from their own organization?', N'Before the session: bring one deal with a commercial structure that needs approving.', N'Which approvals does this structure trigger, and who owns each?
Which are genuinely sequential and which have you been treating as sequential out of habit?
What does the partner have to obtain that you cannot?', N'Listen for approval owners named by role rather than by person, for parallel approvals treated as a chain, for partner-side steps assumed to be quick, and for evidence requirements discovered at submission.', N'Move from the mapped path to the one approval each person will start today.
Customer Zero cue - use only if it happens: when someone reverts, rejects or overrides what the agent produced, stop and name it. Ask what the agent could not see. Huddle transcripts are reviewed for themes, so what is said here does reach us - keep customer-confidential detail out of it.', N'If nobody has a live deal, reconstruct the approval path of the last one that slipped and identify where the time went.', N'Close by naming the approval each person is starting and what evidence it needs.', N'Which approval did you assume somebody else was handling?', N'Before the next huddle, map the approval path for one deal with a named owner and the required evidence for each step, mark which belong to the partner, and start the one on the critical path.', N'Bring the map and what you found was missing from it.'),
        (N'FG-SMEC-CE-DEAL2', N'WF-X-DEAL-01', N'SMEC-CE-DEAL2', N'A commercial structure that cannot be explained without you in the room will not get approved when you are not. This session is about making one reviewable.', N'In this segment the CE is frequently absent from the conversation where the structure is actually decided - the partner is in the room and the approver reads a document. That makes the written case the product, not a summary of it. Separate the three things an approver needs: what decision they are being asked to make, whether the numbers can be checked, and what is being asserted without support. Facilitator note: the instinct is to make the case persuasive. Push the group towards making it inspectable instead, because an approver who cannot verify a figure will not approve around it.', N'When did an approval last stall on something other than the merits?
What in your last commercial case could the approver not have checked?
Who has to be able to read this when you are not there?', N'Before the session: bring one commercial structure you have agreed and still need to take to approval.', N'What exactly is the approver being asked to decide?
Which figure in this could someone else not verify?
What have you asserted that you have not shown?', N'Listen for cases that argue rather than evidence, for figures whose source nobody can name, for judgments stated as facts, and for people describing what they would say in the room when they will not be in the room.', N'Move from the narrative to the one gap each person will close before sending it.', N'If nobody has a live structure awaiting approval, use the last one that was approved and ask what the approver had to take on trust.', N'Close by naming the single unevidenced claim each person is going to close before their case goes out.', N'What in your last approval request did the approver have to take on trust?', N'Before the next huddle, write the decision narrative for one commercial structure, have it read back for unsupported claims, and close the gap on the central one.', N'Bring the ask as you stated it, the gap that came back, and what you changed.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-ARCH', N'WF-X-ARCH-01', N'DCSA-ARCH', N'Architecture in this segment is judged by whether it still works six months after you have moved on. This session is about designing for that.', N'A DCSA carries a portfolio of non-unified customers - no dedicated support relationship, often no in-house platform team, and frequently a partner doing the implementation. That changes what a good design is. The elegant option that assumes someone will tune it is worse than the plain option that runs untouched. Separate the design decisions that need your expertise from the ones the customer or partner will end up making anyway, and make the second group hard to get wrong. Facilitator note: the instinct is to compare architectures on capability. Push the group to compare them on what happens when nobody is watching.', N'Who is actually going to build this?
What does this design assume somebody will maintain?
What breaks first if nobody touches it for six months?', N'Before the session: bring one architecture you are designing or reviewing now, and know who will implement it.', N'What does this design assume about the people running it?
Which decision here did you make because it was right, and which because it was familiar?
What would you simplify if you knew nobody would ever tune it?', N'Listen for designs justified by capability rather than by operability, for assumptions about customer skill that nobody has tested, for a partner implementer who has not been consulted, and for support models being assumed rather than confirmed.', N'Move from the design to the single assumption each person will go and verify.', N'If nobody has a live design, take an architecture already deployed on one of their accounts and ask what it assumed that turned out not to be true.', N'Close by naming the one operability assumption each person will confirm with the implementer this week.', N'Which of your designs needed you more than it should have?', N'Before the next huddle, take one design to the person who will build it and confirm they can operate it without you.', N'Bring the assumption you tested and what the implementer said.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-HEALTH', N'WF-X-HEALTH-01', N'DCSA-HEALTH', N'Every consumption problem has a technical cause that appeared first. This session is about catching it at that point rather than at the review.', N'Across a portfolio of this size nobody can inspect accounts individually, so technical risk is found either by a signal or by a complaint. The gap between those two is where the DCSA earns their place. Distinguish three things that look identical from outside: a workload that was never fully deployed, one that was deployed and abandoned, and one that is working but small. Only the first two are yours. Facilitator note: this session pairs with a standing check - the point is not the one-off scan but the rhythm that replaces it.', N'When did you last find a technical problem before the customer did?
What told you about your last consumption drop, and how late was it?
Which of your accounts could be in trouble without you knowing?', N'Before the session: know which consumption or usage view you can actually reach for your portfolio.', N'What is the earliest signal you could realistically have seen?
Which risks here would the customer dispute?
What would you want to be told automatically every week?', N'Listen for risk lists with no owner, for signals being read as problems without a technical hypothesis, for people describing manual checks they know they will not sustain, and for accounts nobody has looked at in months being called healthy.', N'Move from the scan to the standing check that means they do not have to remember to run it.', N'If consumption data is not reachable, use support and service records instead and ask what those alone can and cannot tell you.', N'Close by having each person name the risk they are acting on and confirm their standing check is running.', N'What did you find late that you could have found early?', N'Before the next huddle, start one technical intervention and leave a standing check running on your portfolio.', N'Bring the risk you acted on, what the check has surfaced since, and whether any of it was new.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-EXPAND', N'WF-X-RENEW-01', N'DCSA-EXPAND', N'Expansion in this segment is found in the telemetry of what is already working, not built in a deck. This session is about reading that evidence.', N'A DCSA is accountable for uncovering the next logical workload, but not for selling it - that boundary matters and gets blurred under pressure. Readiness has three parts and all three have to hold: the customer is succeeding with something adjacent, they have the technical capacity to take on more, and somebody on their side wants it. Two out of three produces a stalled deployment that damages the account you were trying to grow. Facilitator note: watch for expansion proposed from what Microsoft wants to sell rather than from what the customer has proven they can run.', N'Which of your accounts is genuinely succeeding with something right now?
What did your last expansion attempt assume that was not true?
Who on the customer side would actually want the next workload?', N'Before the session: bring one account where a workload is deployed and working.', N'What is the evidence this customer is ready rather than just suitable?
Who asked for this, or did we?
What would have to be true technically before this could start?', N'Listen for expansion built on product fit rather than customer readiness, for deployed being read as adopted, for no named customer sponsor, and for DCSAs carrying the commercial conversation themselves rather than handing it over.', N'Move from the hypothesis to who owns the conversation and what they need from it.', N'If nobody has a succeeding workload, work the opposite way: take a stalled deployment and ask what would have had to be true for it to have worked.', N'Close by naming the owner of each expansion conversation and what evidence goes with it.', N'Which expansion did you propose that the customer was not ready for?', N'Before the next huddle, evidence one expansion hypothesis and hand it to whoever owns the commercial conversation.', N'Bring the readiness evidence, who you handed it to, and what they did with it.'),
        (N'FG-DSE-ASSET', N'WF-X-ASSET-01', N'DSE-ASSET', N'The same technical problem arrives at this team repeatedly wearing different customer names. This session is about answering it once.', N'Reuse fails for a predictable reason: the asset gets saved and the context does not. Another engineer finds a script or a design and cannot tell what it assumed, so they rebuild it, and now there are two. The reusable unit is the artefact plus its constraints - what it was built for, what it depends on, and where it does not apply. Sanitization matters more here than in Enterprise because at this volume an asset gets reused many times before anyone reviews it. Facilitator note: the community hubs already hold field-contributed assets; check before building.', N'What have you built more than once?
What have you looked for and failed to find?
What would somebody need to know before reusing your last piece of work?', N'Before the session: bring one artefact you built recently that you think somebody else could use.', N'Could somebody use this without talking to you?
What does it assume that is not written down?
Where would this be wrong to apply?', N'Listen for assets shared without their constraints, for customer-identifying detail left in, for reuse being treated as filing rather than as writing, and for people describing things they rebuilt that already existed somewhere.', N'Move from the artefact to where it will live and who will find it.
Customer Zero cue - use only if it happens: when someone reverts, rejects or overrides what the agent produced, stop and name it. Ask what the agent could not see. Huddle transcripts are reviewed for themes, so what is said here does reach us - keep customer-confidential detail out of it.', N'If nobody has an artefact, use the last question they answered twice and capture the answer instead.', N'Close by confirming where each asset is going and who else has been told it exists.', N'What did you rebuild this quarter that already existed?', N'Before the next huddle, publish one asset with its limitations stated, and tell the team where it is.', N'Bring the asset, where it now lives, and whether anyone has used it.'),
        (N'FG-DSE-PARTNER', N'WF-X-ORCH-01', N'DSE-PARTNER', N'When a partner-led deal stops moving, the cause is usually that somebody on the partner side is not confident enough to take the next technical step. This session is about finding that and fixing it properly.', N'This is the one part of the DSE path with no Enterprise equivalent, and it carries a genuine tension: the fastest way to unblock a partner-led deal is to do the technical work yourself, and that is exactly what prevents the partner ever becoming self-sufficient. The distinction to hold is between a gap in knowledge, which enablement fixes, and a gap in capacity, which it does not. Facilitator note: partner-side detail is limited in our systems, so most of the evidence here comes from what the partner tells you and how their deals actually behave.', N'Which of your partner-led deals keep needing you?
What do you end up explaining more than once?
Is the partner missing knowledge, or missing people?', N'Before the session: bring one partner-led opportunity that has stalled or that keeps returning to you.', N'What exactly can the partner not do yet?
Is that knowledge, capacity, or willingness?
What would let them do this without you next time?', N'Listen for DSEs absorbing partner work and calling it enablement, for capability gaps described as attitude problems, for enablement with no owner or date, and for the same partner appearing behind several stalled deals.', N'Move from the gap to the specific enablement action and who owns it on the partner side.', N'If nobody has a stalled partner deal, use the last one they unblocked and ask what would have happened if they had been unavailable.', N'Close by naming the enablement action, its owner on the partner side, and the date.', N'What are you doing for a partner that they should be able to do themselves?', N'Before the next huddle, name one partner technical gap and agree an enablement action with a named owner and a date.', N'Bring the gap, the action agreed, and whether the partner did it.'),
        (N'FG-DSS-COMP', N'WF-X-DEAL-01', N'DSS-COMP', N'Competitive positions usually fail because they answer a comparison the customer was not making. This session is about finding the real one.', N'A DSS carries a solution area across many accounts, so the same competitive conversation recurs with small variations - which makes it worth getting right once. Three things get confused: what we do better, what the customer values, and what they are actually comparing us to. Only the overlap of all three is a differentiator. Being honest about where a competitor is stronger is what makes the rest of the position credible, and sellers consistently underrate how obvious the omission is to a customer who has already seen the other vendor. Facilitator note: push for the specific competitor and the specific criteria, not a general positioning discussion.', N'What is this customer actually comparing us to?
Which of our advantages have they told you they care about?
Where is the competitor genuinely better?', N'Before the session: bring one live opportunity where a competitor is involved and you know who it is.', N'What are the customer''s own decision criteria, in their words?
Which differentiator survives contact with those criteria?
What will you concede, and when?', N'Listen for feature comparisons standing in for customer criteria, for competitor weaknesses asserted without a source, for positions that concede nothing, and for the same objection appearing across several accounts without anyone having prepared for it.', N'Move from the position to the one objection each person will prepare properly.', N'If nobody has a live competitive deal, take the objection they hear most often across the solution area and build the position around that instead.', N'Close by naming the objection each person will prepare and the evidence they will bring to it.', N'Which competitive claim have you made that you could not evidence?', N'Before the next huddle, test one competitive position with a customer and record which part of it actually landed.', N'Bring the position, what the customer responded to, and what you dropped.'),
        (N'FG-DSS-ORCH', N'WF-X-ORCH-01', N'DSS-ORCH', N'In this segment nobody is assigned to your opportunity. Everything you need comes from someone who is also doing their own job. This session is about asking well.', N'An ask that describes a situation gets sympathy; an ask that describes a specific task with a date gets done. The three things that make an ask answerable are what exactly is needed, by when, and what happens if it does not arrive - the last one is almost always omitted and is what moves an ask up someone''s list. Separate asks that need a person from asks that need a decision, because they go to different places and have different lead times. Facilitator note: watch for people who have absorbed a task themselves rather than asking, and ask what that cost them.', N'What are you doing yourself that you should have asked for?
What did you last ask for that never arrived?
Would the person receiving your ask know exactly what to do?', N'Before the session: bring one opportunity where you need support you have not yet asked for.', N'What exactly do you need, and by when?
Who specifically, not which team?
What happens to the deal if it does not come?', N'Listen for asks addressed to a team rather than a person, for needs described as context rather than as tasks, for no date, and for people who have stopped asking because previous asks went unanswered.', N'Move from the ask to the standing check that tells them when it has gone quiet.', N'If nobody has a live need, use the last support ask that went unanswered and rewrite it as it should have been sent.', N'Close by confirming each ask has a person, a task and a date, and that a check is running.', N'What did you stop asking for because it never came?', N'Before the next huddle, send one specific support ask and set a check that tells you if it goes unanswered.', N'Bring the ask, whether it was answered, and how long it took.'),
        (N'FG-PSS-CAPACITY', N'WF-X-HEALTH-01', N'PSS-CAPACITY', N'Partner capability and partner capacity look identical from the outside and fail in completely different ways. This session is about telling them apart before you route work.', N'Capability is whether they know how; capacity is whether they have anyone free. Enablement fixes the first and does nothing for the second, which is why so much partner enablement is politely attended and never applied. The evidence available to us is partial - program participation and past delivery tell you about capability, and almost nothing tells you about capacity, which usually has to be asked. Facilitator note: a partner rarely says no to work, so absence of refusal is not evidence of readiness.', N'Which of your partners have you routed work to without checking?
When a partner-led engagement last went wrong, was it skill or bandwidth?
How would you know a partner was overcommitted?', N'Before the session: bring one partner you are currently routing work to or considering.', N'Is this a knowledge gap or a people gap?
What evidence do you have, and what are you assuming?
What would you have to see to route confidently?', N'Listen for capability judged from the partner''s own claims, for capacity never being asked about, for program certifications treated as delivery evidence, and for partners being chosen by relationship rather than by readiness.', N'Move from the assessment to the specific action and who on the partner side owns it.', N'If nobody has a live routing decision, use a partner engagement that went badly and work out which of the two gaps it actually was.', N'Close by naming the gap, the closing action, its partner-side owner and the date.', N'Which partner have you kept using out of habit rather than evidence?', N'Before the next huddle, assess one partner''s readiness properly and agree one closing action with them.', N'Bring the gap you found, what you agreed, and whether the partner acted.'),
        (N'FG-PSS-CONV', N'WF-X-CONV-01', N'PSS-CONV', N'Partners agree to a great deal in meetings and act on very little of it. The difference is almost always whether the ask made sense in their economics.', N'You influence without authority across more partners than you can meet, so each conversation has to carry weight. A partner''s economics are not a mystery - margin, utilization of their consultants, recurring revenue and how quickly they get paid - and framing an ask against any of those changes the response. The most common failure is a conversation that reports Microsoft''s view of the partnership back to a partner who experiences it completely differently. Facilitator note: Know Your Partner is purpose-built for assembling this briefing; confirm access before relying on it.', N'What does this partner actually make money on?
What did you last ask a partner to do, and did they?
What would they say the partnership is currently worth to them?', N'Before the session: bring one partner you have a conversation coming up with.', N'What is in this for their business specifically?
What is your one ask?
What will they raise that you have not prepared for?', N'Listen for asks framed in Microsoft targets, for partner economics being assumed rather than known, for several asks where there should be one, and for briefings that report performance without proposing anything.', N'Move from the briefing to rehearsing the one ask and the objection they expect.', N'If Know Your Partner is not available to you, assemble the same evidence from partner performance reporting and use the rest of the workflow unchanged.', N'Close by confirming each person has one ask, framed in partner economics, and has rehearsed the objection.', N'When did a partner agree with you and then not act?', N'Before the next huddle, have one partner conversation with a single ask framed in their economics, and record what they committed to.', N'Bring the ask, what they committed to, and whether it happened.'),
        (N'FG-DSS-INVEST', N'WF-INVEST-01', N'DSS-INVEST', N'Investment in SME&C is not one thing. This session is about picking the right route before you build anything.', N'Investment in this segment is a selection problem before it is a submission problem. There are several routes - direct customer investment, partner-led programs, and voucher-based offers - and most sellers learn one and use it for everything. The FY27 SME&C customer investment analysis records that Azure post-sales investment expects first-year ACR above roughly 500,000 US dollars, and that below that threshold the motion routes partner-led; it also records vouchers as the scale vehicle, at around 20,000 US dollars for Corporate against 50,000 for Enterprise. Treat those as orientation from that analysis rather than as current policy - confirm any figure with the authoritative owner before it reaches a customer. The point of the session is that choosing the wrong route wastes weeks that the right route would not have cost. For a DSS the relevant question is usually whether the solution-area opportunity is large enough to carry direct investment or whether it belongs in a partner-led or voucher motion. Facilitator note: resist letting this become an ECIF mechanics session - the mechanics only matter once the route is right.', N'Which investment route do you reach for by default?
When did an investment request last fail, and at what stage?
Who tells you whether something is eligible?', N'Before the session: bring one solution-area opportunity where customer investment might help.', N'What is the barrier the investment would actually remove?
Which route fits the size and shape of this deal?
What do you not know about eligibility?', N'Listen for one route being used for everything, for eligibility being assumed from a previous deal, for investment proposed before the barrier is clear, and for people who have stopped requesting because a past request failed late.', N'Move from the route to the eligibility question each person will route to an owner.
Customer Zero cue - use only if it happens: when someone reverts, rejects or overrides what the agent produced, stop and name it. Ask what the agent could not see. Huddle transcripts are reviewed for themes, so what is said here does reach us - keep customer-confidential detail out of it.', N'If nobody has a live candidate, take the last investment request that failed and work out which route it should have taken.', N'Close by confirming each person has a route and one eligibility question sent to a named owner.', N'What did you last build that turned out to be the wrong vehicle?', N'Before the next huddle, match one opportunity to an investment route and get one eligibility question answered authoritatively.', N'Bring the route you chose, the question you asked and the answer you got.'),
        (N'FG-PSS-INVEST', N'WF-INVEST-01', N'PSS-INVEST', N'The investment routes that matter most in this segment run through partners. This session is about using them deliberately.', N'Investment in this segment is a selection problem before it is a submission problem. There are several routes - direct customer investment, partner-led programs, and voucher-based offers - and most sellers learn one and use it for everything. The FY27 SME&C customer investment analysis records that Azure post-sales investment expects first-year ACR above roughly 500,000 US dollars, and that below that threshold the motion routes partner-led; it also records vouchers as the scale vehicle, at around 20,000 US dollars for Corporate against 50,000 for Enterprise. Treat those as orientation from that analysis rather than as current policy - confirm any figure with the authoritative owner before it reaches a customer. The point of the session is that choosing the wrong route wastes weeks that the right route would not have cost. For a PSS the additional dimension is that partner-led routes place obligations on the PARTNER - nomination, activity completion, evidence - and a partner who does not know what they have signed up to is the most common reason a partner-led investment produces nothing. Facilitator note: the partner''s incentive under each route is worth naming explicitly; it is rarely the same as ours.', N'Which partner-led routes do you actually use?
When did a partner-led program last produce nothing, and why?
Does the partner know what the program requires of them?', N'Before the session: bring one partner and one opportunity where investment could help.', N'What does this route require the partner to do?
What does the partner get out of it?
Who is tracking whether they do it?', N'Listen for partner obligations being assumed rather than agreed, for programs nominated and never started, for the partner''s incentive being taken for granted, and for PSS carrying activity that the program expects the partner to carry.', N'Move from the route to the specific obligation the partner has agreed to and the date.', N'If nobody has a live candidate, use a partner program that was nominated and never completed and work out where it stalled.', N'Close by confirming each person has a route and a partner-side obligation agreed with a name and a date.', N'Which partner program did you nominate and never follow up?', N'Before the next huddle, match one partner opportunity to a route and confirm with the partner what it requires of them.', N'Bring the route, what the partner agreed to, and whether they started.'),
        (N'FG-DAE-INVEST', N'WF-INVEST-01', N'DAE-INVEST', N'Customer investment reaches your role even though you do not own the request. This session is about the part that is yours.', N'Investment in this segment is a selection problem before it is a submission problem. There are several routes - direct customer investment, partner-led programs, and voucher-based offers - and most sellers learn one and use it for everything. The FY27 SME&C customer investment analysis records that Azure post-sales investment expects first-year ACR above roughly 500,000 US dollars, and that below that threshold the motion routes partner-led; it also records vouchers as the scale vehicle, at around 20,000 US dollars for Corporate against 50,000 for Enterprise. Treat those as orientation from that analysis rather than as current policy - confirm any figure with the authoritative owner before it reaches a customer. The point of the session is that choosing the wrong route wastes weeks that the right route would not have cost. A DAE does not own investment requests but meets the barriers that investment removes, across more accounts than anyone else. The value is in recognition and routing rather than in submission. Facilitator note: keep this session on the role''s own contribution rather than letting it drift into submission mechanics owned elsewhere.', N'Where have you seen investment help, and where have you seen it wasted?
What does your role actually contribute to an investment decision?
Who owns the request once you have raised it?', N'Before the session: bring one situation where customer investment is or was in play.', N'What is the barrier, precisely?
What is yours to decide here and what is not?
What would you need to see to be confident?', N'Listen for investment proposed as a discount by another name, for barriers that are really timing, and for DAEs building requests themselves rather than routing them.', N'Move from the assessment to the one thing each person routes or confirms this week.', N'If nobody has a live case, use the last investment situation they encountered and work through what their role should have contributed.', N'Close by naming what each person will route or confirm, and to whom.', N'When did you last see investment used for something it could not fix?', N'Before the next huddle, take one investment situation and contribute the part your role owns.', N'Bring what you found, who you took it to, and what happened.'),
        (N'FG-DSE-INVEST', N'WF-INVEST-01', N'DSE-INVEST', N'Customer investment reaches your role even though you do not own the request. This session is about the part that is yours.', N'Investment in this segment is a selection problem before it is a submission problem. There are several routes - direct customer investment, partner-led programs, and voucher-based offers - and most sellers learn one and use it for everything. The FY27 SME&C customer investment analysis records that Azure post-sales investment expects first-year ACR above roughly 500,000 US dollars, and that below that threshold the motion routes partner-led; it also records vouchers as the scale vehicle, at around 20,000 US dollars for Corporate against 50,000 for Enterprise. Treat those as orientation from that analysis rather than as current policy - confirm any figure with the authoritative owner before it reaches a customer. The point of the session is that choosing the wrong route wastes weeks that the right route would not have cost. A DSE does not own the investment request but owns whether the technical work it funds proves anything. Criteria set afterwards are always contested. Facilitator note: keep this session on the role''s own contribution rather than letting it drift into submission mechanics owned elsewhere.', N'Where have you seen investment help, and where have you seen it wasted?
What does your role actually contribute to an investment decision?
Who owns the request once you have raised it?', N'Before the session: bring one situation where customer investment is or was in play.', N'What is the barrier, precisely?
What is yours to decide here and what is not?
What would you need to see to be confident?', N'Listen for success criteria nobody could observe, for scope that expands to fill the funding, and for exit criteria being omitted entirely.', N'Move from the assessment to the one thing each person routes or confirms this week.', N'If nobody has a live case, use the last investment situation they encountered and work through what their role should have contributed.', N'Close by naming what each person will route or confirm, and to whom.', N'When did you last see investment used for something it could not fix?', N'Before the next huddle, take one investment situation and contribute the part your role owns.', N'Bring what you found, who you took it to, and what happened.'),
        (N'FG-DCSA-INVEST', N'WF-INVEST-01', N'DCSA-INVEST', N'Customer investment reaches your role even though you do not own the request. This session is about the part that is yours.', N'Investment in this segment is a selection problem before it is a submission problem. There are several routes - direct customer investment, partner-led programs, and voucher-based offers - and most sellers learn one and use it for everything. The FY27 SME&C customer investment analysis records that Azure post-sales investment expects first-year ACR above roughly 500,000 US dollars, and that below that threshold the motion routes partner-led; it also records vouchers as the scale vehicle, at around 20,000 US dollars for Corporate against 50,000 for Enterprise. Treat those as orientation from that analysis rather than as current policy - confirm any figure with the authoritative owner before it reaches a customer. The point of the session is that choosing the wrong route wastes weeks that the right route would not have cost. A DCSA does not own the investment request but is accountable for whether the funded work converts into consumption. Non-unified customers have less capacity to absorb delivery than the request usually assumes. Facilitator note: keep this session on the role''s own contribution rather than letting it drift into submission mechanics owned elsewhere.', N'Where have you seen investment help, and where have you seen it wasted?
What does your role actually contribute to an investment decision?
Who owns the request once you have raised it?', N'Before the session: bring one situation where customer investment is or was in play.', N'What is the barrier, precisely?
What is yours to decide here and what is not?
What would you need to see to be confident?', N'Listen for prerequisites the customer has been told about but not agreed to, for partner delivery capacity being assumed, and for readiness judged from the opportunity rather than the environment.', N'Move from the assessment to the one thing each person routes or confirms this week.', N'If nobody has a live case, use the last investment situation they encountered and work through what their role should have contributed.', N'Close by naming what each person will route or confirm, and to whom.', N'When did you last see investment used for something it could not fix?', N'Before the next huddle, take one investment situation and contribute the part your role owns.', N'Bring what you found, who you took it to, and what happened.'),
        (N'FG-SMEC-CE-INVEST', N'WF-INVEST-01', N'SMEC-CE-INVEST', N'This is a short awareness session rather than a workflow. The CE does not own investment requests in this segment, but investment lands on deals you structure.', N'Investment in this segment is a selection problem before it is a submission problem. There are several routes - direct customer investment, partner-led programs, and voucher-based offers - and most sellers learn one and use it for everything. The FY27 SME&C customer investment analysis records that Azure post-sales investment expects first-year ACR above roughly 500,000 US dollars, and that below that threshold the motion routes partner-led; it also records vouchers as the scale vehicle, at around 20,000 US dollars for Corporate against 50,000 for Enterprise. Treat those as orientation from that analysis rather than as current policy - confirm any figure with the authoritative owner before it reaches a customer. The point of the session is that choosing the wrong route wastes weeks that the right route would not have cost. No source assigns the SME&C Commercial Executive ownership of investment requests, which is why this is an awareness card rather than a full workflow. What IS yours: whether an investment changes the commercial structure, whether it creates a precedent that the next similar customer will expect, and whether the structure still works if the investment is declined. Facilitator note: keep this short and do not let it become an ECIF mechanics session - route those questions to the roles that own them.', N'When has an investment changed a structure you built?
What would you want to know before structuring around one?
Who would you ask?', N'Before the session: think of one deal where investment was involved.', N'What would change about the structure if the investment did not come?
What precedent does this set?
Whose decision is the investment itself?', N'Listen for CEs taking on investment requests that belong elsewhere, for structures that only work if investment is approved, and for precedent implications going unnoticed.', N'Move from the discussion to the single question each person would raise next time.', N'If nobody has an example, use a deal with a non-standard term and ask what would have changed if investment had been part of it.', N'Close by naming the one question each person would raise if investment appeared on a deal they were structuring.', N'Have you ever built a structure that depended on funding you did not control?', N'Before the next huddle, if investment appears on a deal you are structuring, test whether the structure still holds without it.', N'Bring the deal, the dependency you found, and what you did about it.'),
        (N'FG-ALL-CLOSEDWON', N'WF-X-PIPE-01', N'ALL-CLOSEDWON', N'A closed-won record carries your number whether or not you were the one who set it. Closes arrive through your own work, through automation, and through other people acting on the account. This session is about reading your own recent closes and deciding which ones you can stand behind.', N'A wrong close raises no error and blocks nothing. It stays in the book until somebody has to explain it, and by then the context has gone.
The test is not whether the record is well-formed. It is whether the value, the products and the date describe what was actually sold.
Your account knowledge is the input here, not the verdict. A correction made without the contract in front of you replaces one wrong record with another.
Finding nothing is a real result. Say so in the room rather than treating the exercise as having failed.', N'When did you last read your own closed-won list rather than your open pipeline?
If a close in your book were wrong, how would you find out?
Which part of a closed record would you struggle to explain in a review?', N'Before the session: confirm you can open Sales Agent, and choose a recent period short enough to read in one sitting. Bring what you remember about the accounts in it, because that memory is the instrument this exercise runs on.', N'Which close in your list did you not recognise straight away?
What in that record does not match what you remember selling?
Is this one yours to correct, or does it need somebody else?', N'Listen for sellers scrolling past records they do not recognise because the totals look right. Listen for “that must have been the partner” or “that came from the team” offered as an explanation rather than as something to check. Listen for reluctance to raise a close that flatters the number, and for the assumption that a well-formed record is a correct one.', N'Move from the list of closes nobody could account for to the single record each person will resolve, and how they will resolve it.', N'If Sales Agent access is not available, run it from a report export or the opportunity list, since the exercise is reading your own closes rather than the tool that surfaces them. If nobody finds an unexplained close, use the time to ask how they would notice one.', N'Close by having each person name the one record they will resolve, and which of the four it is: accepted, corrected by them, routed to somebody who can act on it, or held pending a specific check.', N'What did you find in your own closed book that you had not looked at before? What would have to be true for you to notice it sooner next time?', N'Before the next huddle, resolve one closed-won record you could not account for: accept it, correct it, route it to whoever can act on it, or write down the specific thing you need to check.', N'Bring the record, what you could not account for, what you did about it, and whether it turned out to be yours to correct.'),
        (N'FG-CSA-VSAT', N'WF-X-VSAT-01', N'CSA-VSAT', N'Satisfaction is decided during the delivery, not at the survey. Today is about reading it early enough to act.', N'The customer already knows how they feel. The survey only tells us.
Responsiveness, clarity and expectation-setting move scores as much as technical outcomes do.
A satisfaction risk you cannot evidence is a feeling. Name what you saw.', N'Where in a current delivery would the customer say we are harder to work with than we need to be?
Which satisfaction signal have you noticed and not acted on?
What would this customer say if they were asked today rather than at the end?', N'Before the session: bring one live delivery, any customer feedback or escalation on it, and the moment you would least like the customer to remember.', N'Which delivery are you least confident about, and what is the evidence rather than the feeling?
What would the customer say we did slowly or explained badly?
Which satisfaction risk is yours to fix and which belongs to another role?', N'Through the CSA lens, listen for: satisfaction inferred from the absence of complaints; technical success treated as the whole of the customer experience; risks named without a specific moment behind them; and anything the CSA plans to absorb that belongs to the CSAM or to delivery.', N'Move from the general idea of satisfaction to one live delivery and the specific moment that would show up in the score.', N'Use a sanitized delivery. Do not speculate about individual customer contacts or attribute dissatisfaction to named people.', N'Name one satisfaction risk, the evidence behind it, and the action you will take before the delivery closes.', N'What did you learn today? Name one thing AI made easier, one thing that still needed your judgment, and one thing you would do differently next time.', N'Take one live delivery, name the satisfaction risk you would least like to see in the survey, and act on it this week.', N'Bring the risk you named and what changed after you acted.'),
        (N'FG-CSA-TECHLEAD', N'WF-X-TECHLEAD-01', N'CSA-TECHLEAD', N'You get called in when you are the person who can answer the question. Today is about deciding which questions those will be.', N'Technical credibility is set by the customer''s roadmap, not by our product releases.
The gap worth closing is the one the customer hits next, not the most interesting one.
Being able to say what you do not know is part of being trusted, not the opposite of it.', N'What will this customer be building in six months that you could not advise on today?
Where have you been the bottleneck because the answer had to come from someone else?
Which technical topic do you avoid, and what does avoiding it cost?', N'Before the session: bring one customer, what you know of their technical direction, and one question you were recently unable to answer.', N'What is the next technical decision this customer faces, and could you hold that conversation today?
Which gap is real and which is imagined?
What is the smallest step that would make you credible on it?', N'Through the CSA lens, listen for: gaps chosen by personal interest rather than by customer need; plans to learn everything rather than one thing; and cases where the honest answer is to bring in a specialist rather than close the gap personally.', N'Move from general capability to one customer, one upcoming technical decision, and one gap.', N'Use a sanitized customer. Keep the focus on technical direction rather than commercial detail.', N'Name the technical gap you are closing, why this customer makes it urgent, and the first step.', N'What did you learn today? Name one thing AI made easier, one thing that still needed your judgment, and one thing you would do differently next time.', N'Name one technical topic your customer will need you on within the next two quarters, and take the first step toward being able to defend it.', N'Bring the gap you named and what you did about it.')
    ) v([ExternalId], [TopicExternalId], [PlacementExternalId], [SessionIntroduction], [KeyTalkingPoints], [DiscussionQuestions], [PreparationChecklist], [FacilitatorQuestions], [ListenFor], [SuggestedTransitions], [FallbackGuidance], [WrapUpGuidance], [ReflectPrompt], [CommitPrompt], [BringBackEvidence])
)
SELECT @unresolved = 35 - COUNT(*) FROM raw
    INNER JOIN dbo.HuddleTopics AS t ON t.[ExternalId] = raw.[TopicExternalId]
    INNER JOIN dbo.HuddlePlacements AS p ON p.[ExternalId] = raw.[PlacementExternalId];
IF @unresolved <> 0
    THROW 51000, 'HuddleFacilitatorGuides (new): 35 source rows were expected to resolve their foreign keys but some did not. Run the earlier scripts in order and re-check the workbook.', 1;

